WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.938
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4532.

2
00:00:00.938 --> 00:00:01.875
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:01.875 --> 00:00:02.188
<v STEPHANIE DRAGOON>Okay.

4
00:00:02.188 --> 00:00:02.500
Great.

5
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:04.062
It is October tenth, 2015.

6
00:00:04.062 --> 00:00:07.188
We are at Ms. Margaret’s home in Lake Charles [Louisiana].

7
00:00:07.188 --> 00:00:12.812
I’m Stephanie Dragoon with the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

8
00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:17.188
I’m here today with Ms. Margaret LaTour-Pitre to talk about her memories of Mossville,

9
00:00:17.188 --> 00:00:23.438
Louisiana as part of a project conducted in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville.

10
00:00:23.438 --> 00:00:29.062
Thank you so much for your time and for inviting us to your home here in Lake Charles.

11
00:00:29.062 --> 00:00:29.688
<v ENOLA MARGARET LATOUR-PITRE>You’re welcome.

12
00:00:29.688 --> 00:00:31.875
<v DRAGOON>This is our first interview session together.

13
00:00:31.875 --> 00:00:35.000
Could you please state your name for the recording?

14
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.402
<v LATOUR-PITRE>First name Enola, Margaret LaTour-Pitre.

15
00:00:37.402 --> 00:00:40.284
<v DRAGOON>When and where were you born?

16
00:00:40.284 --> 00:00:45.088
<v LATOUR-PITRE>I was born in Lafayette, Louisiana on November sixteenth, 1946.

17
00:00:45.088 --> 00:00:47.490
<v DRAGOON>What were your parents’ names?

18
00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:50.373
<v LATOUR-PITRE>My father’s name was Raymond LaTour.

19
00:00:50.373 --> 00:00:54.216
My mother’s name was Lily May Guillory LaTour.

20
00:00:54.216 --> 00:00:58.059
<v DRAGOON>And what did they do for a living?

21
00:00:58.059 --> 00:01:06.225
<v LATOUR-PITRE>My father was a laborer, I guess, and then trained to become a bricklayer and cement finisher.

22
00:01:06.225 --> 00:01:11.510
And my mother was . . . she was a housekeeper for a while.

23
00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:14.392
But then mostly her own family.

24
00:01:14.392 --> 00:01:19.196
A domestic, I guess, they called them at the time.

25
00:01:19.196 --> 00:01:24.000
But she worked quite often when we were young.

26
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.786
<v DRAGOON>And where did she work?

27
00:01:25.786 --> 00:01:34.357
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Like I said, she was a housekeeper for various people in the community, the various people that she would work for in different places.

28
00:01:34.357 --> 00:01:35.071
<v DRAGOON>Around Mossville?

29
00:01:35.071 --> 00:01:35.786
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Sulphur [Louisiana].

30
00:01:35.786 --> 00:01:37.929
Yeah, mostly it was in Sulphur.

31
00:01:37.929 --> 00:01:42.571
Some . . . I think it was a few of them in Lake Charles [Louisiana].

32
00:01:42.571 --> 00:01:45.786
But most of the jobs were Sulphur and Maplewood.

33
00:01:45.786 --> 00:01:47.929
Maplewood was booming at the time.

34
00:01:47.929 --> 00:01:51.143
So there were some people she worked for long-term.

35
00:01:51.143 --> 00:01:54.000
<v DRAGOON>Can you tell me about your grandparents?

36
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.000
<v LATOUR-PITRE>My grandfather on my father’s side was Joe LaTour.

37
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:03.000
He actually, when I was very young, I guess somewhere in the early ‘50s, had had a stroke.

38
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.333
He was living out in the country near Oberlin [Louisiana],

39
00:02:06.333 --> 00:02:11.000
but had had a stroke and was sort of disabled . . . or handicapped, at least.

40
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:15.333
They built a house right next door to ours, to my family home.

41
00:02:15.333 --> 00:02:20.667
So from the early ‘50s until not long ago, when the house was finally taken down,

42
00:02:20.667 --> 00:02:25.667
my grandfather and his family, second family, moved there and lived next door to us.

43
00:02:25.667 --> 00:02:26.333
<v DRAGOON>In Mossville?

44
00:02:26.333 --> 00:02:27.000
<v LATOUR-PITRE>In Mossville.

45
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.667
They were right next door to the house.

46
00:02:29.667 --> 00:02:34.000
My paternal grandmother had died when my dad was about nine years old.

47
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.333
So my grandfather had remarried and had a second family.

48
00:02:37.333 --> 00:02:41.000
So he and his second family lived next door to us.

49
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:43.000
<v DRAGOON>What street was that on?

50
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:47.529
<v LATOUR-PITRE>When we first moved there, it was called Old Spanish Trail.

51
00:02:47.529 --> 00:02:51.235
But then after a while it was called Burton.

52
00:02:51.235 --> 00:02:54.941
And now that’s the name of it, is Burton.

53
00:02:54.941 --> 00:02:59.882
<v DRAGOON>So a section of the Old Spanish Trail was renamed to Burton?

54
00:02:59.882 --> 00:03:00.294
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

55
00:03:00.294 --> 00:03:03.588
<v DRAGOON>Do you know what year, around, that happened?

56
00:03:03.588 --> 00:03:05.235
<v LATOUR-PITRE>No, actually, I don’t.

57
00:03:05.235 --> 00:03:12.647
But it was Old Spanish Trail until I was probably at least ten, eleven years old or something.

58
00:03:12.647 --> 00:03:15.529
And then they renamed it to Burton.

59
00:03:15.529 --> 00:03:15.941
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

60
00:03:15.941 --> 00:03:17.588
Do you have siblings?

61
00:03:17.588 --> 00:03:20.471
<v LATOUR-PITRE>I have . . . Yes, I have four siblings.

62
00:03:20.471 --> 00:03:23.353
I have one sister and three brothers.

63
00:03:23.353 --> 00:03:25.824
The oldest brother was Raymond Emil.

64
00:03:25.824 --> 00:03:28.294
And then I’m the second child.

65
00:03:28.294 --> 00:03:30.765
And my sister’s right after me.

66
00:03:30.765 --> 00:03:32.412
Her name is Cecilia.

67
00:03:32.412 --> 00:03:36.118
Hopefully you’ll get a chance to talk to them.

68
00:03:36.118 --> 00:03:43.118
And then about nine years after Cecilia, my mother had another boy, and his name is Patrick.

69
00:03:43.118 --> 00:03:48.471
And then two years after him, there was the last, the youngest brother.

70
00:03:48.471 --> 00:03:52.176
So we always joke about the two separate families.

71
00:03:52.176 --> 00:03:57.941
After so many years we ended up with two little brothers in the household.

72
00:03:57.941 --> 00:04:00.000
So that was fun.

73
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.538
<v DRAGOON>What’s the age difference?

74
00:04:01.538 --> 00:04:05.385
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Between myself and the first one was about ten years.

75
00:04:05.385 --> 00:04:09.615
I was just about ten years old when he was born.

76
00:04:09.615 --> 00:04:12.692
And then the last one was twelve years.

77
00:04:12.692 --> 00:04:18.846
I tease them sometimes about having to take care of them, which we did back then.

78
00:04:18.846 --> 00:04:25.000
Having to feed them and give them baths and answer to their needs and their whims.

79
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:27.692
So we joke about that a lot.

80
00:04:27.692 --> 00:04:31.538
<v DRAGOON>So you helped with childcare when you were growing up?

81
00:04:31.538 --> 00:04:36.154
<v LATOUR-PITRE>[Agrees] Like I said, especially when the last two boys came along.

82
00:04:36.154 --> 00:04:36.923
We did.

83
00:04:36.923 --> 00:04:40.000
<v DRAGOON>When did your family move to Mossville?

84
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:42.196
<v LATOUR-PITRE>My brother was born in ‘44.

85
00:04:42.196 --> 00:04:49.150
So . . . And then I was born in, so I think it was about ’45, maybe, when they moved there.

86
00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:52.444
I do have the abstracts with some exact dates.

87
00:04:52.444 --> 00:04:55.373
But I’m trying to serve my memory now.

88
00:04:55.373 --> 00:05:00.497
Because when they originally moved into Mossville, they lived in the community further down,

89
00:05:00.497 --> 00:05:06.353
where the Mossville proper is considered around Prater Road and back toward Sulphur, in that area.

90
00:05:06.353 --> 00:05:12.941
My mother had a brother who . . . I’m not quite sure the history of how he got the house.

91
00:05:12.941 --> 00:05:19.529
He had been in the military, and I guess after World War Two he was looking for property.

92
00:05:19.529 --> 00:05:24.654
He had a house in Mossville and decided he wasn’t going to live there.

93
00:05:24.654 --> 00:05:29.046
So he let one of his brothers live there for a while.

94
00:05:29.046 --> 00:05:31.242
When that brother decided to move,

95
00:05:31.242 --> 00:05:39.294
my parents got married and they were looking for a place to move so they moved and lived there for a while.

96
00:05:39.294 --> 00:05:46.614
So I’m imagining they probably stayed there maybe two or three years, because in the process, they acquired the land.

97
00:05:46.614 --> 00:05:54.301
They bought the land further down where our house is now, and then started getting the lumber and building the house.

98
00:05:54.301 --> 00:05:59.425
So somewhere, I guess, around ’46, and again, the loan is in the abstract,

99
00:05:59.425 --> 00:06:04.183
‘47 and ‘48 was when the house was built and we moved in.

100
00:06:04.183 --> 00:06:06.745
So, yeah, somewhere between ‘46 and ‘48.

101
00:06:06.745 --> 00:06:11.137
Because I remember the stories about my youngest brother, my oldest brother,

102
00:06:11.137 --> 00:06:18.092
being three and four years old and trying to climb a ladder to go help Dad do the roofing.

103
00:06:18.092 --> 00:06:21.386
And that’s one of the stories I remember hearing.

104
00:06:21.386 --> 00:06:26.876
So he was still a little baby, and I’m two years younger than he is.

105
00:06:26.876 --> 00:06:32.000
So it was about ‘48, I think, when they finally settled in there.

106
00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:34.205
<v DRAGOON>And where were they living before?

107
00:06:34.205 --> 00:06:37.880
<v LATOUR-PITRE>I’m not quite sure where the location was in Mossville.

108
00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:46.333
But like I said . . . Well, when they first got married, they were living out in, what we call, in the country, near Basile.

109
00:06:46.333 --> 00:06:53.684
My dad was in a community area between Oberlin and Mamou and Basile, out in one of those rural areas.

110
00:06:53.684 --> 00:06:59.197
And then when he and my mom were married, and she lived in Basile proper.

111
00:06:59.197 --> 00:07:05.812
When they got married, they were like somewhere in between the two places and then, like I said,

112
00:07:05.812 --> 00:07:13.162
relocated to this area, primarily because of the job opportunities that were developing at the time right after the war.

113
00:07:13.162 --> 00:07:15.000
<v DRAGOON>What kinds of opportunities?

114
00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:16.883
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Well, there were just jobs.

115
00:07:16.883 --> 00:07:18.390
There were jobs available.

116
00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:27.805
So when my dad, like I said, when they first moved down here, he worked at a lumber yard, I think in the Dequincy area.

117
00:07:27.805 --> 00:07:32.701
And some of those, the pictures, are in there that you’ll see, later.

118
00:07:32.701 --> 00:07:37.974
But he worked in a lumber yard at first, when he first moved here.

119
00:07:37.974 --> 00:07:43.623
Then he started doing construction type jobs, buildings, and building jobs as a construction worker.

120
00:07:43.623 --> 00:07:47.013
Again, that’s one of the family jokes we had.

121
00:07:47.013 --> 00:07:56.052
Because Mom, as a housekeeper, she’d be out to work and a shower would come up and when we’d look up Dad was home,

122
00:07:56.052 --> 00:08:04.338
and it was like, oh, rats. [laughs] He had the opportunity to be home a lot of times when she was working.

123
00:08:04.338 --> 00:08:09.610
And he did that until . . . so I guess around ‘48, ‘49, until about ’56.

124
00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:13.377
In 1956, someone who knew him got him into Firestone.

125
00:08:13.377 --> 00:08:20.909
Around that timeframe of ‘56, I think, the year when Patrick was born, he was offered a job at Firestone.

126
00:08:20.909 --> 00:08:23.922
And took the job and retired from there.

127
00:08:23.922 --> 00:08:27.312
I think Mom was just one of those high-

128
00:08:27.312 --> 00:08:34.091
<c.p>energy people that she just, she worked, she worked pretty much the whole time we were growing up.

129
00:08:34.091 --> 00:08:36.727
But her priority was always her family.

130
00:08:36.727 --> 00:08:39.364
Definitely always her kids and her household.

131
00:08:39.364 --> 00:08:42.000
<v DRAGOON>What’s your earliest memory of Mossville?

132
00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:48.692
<v LATOUR-PITRE>My earliest one is, again, because I was so young when we actually moved on that end of town,

133
00:08:48.692 --> 00:08:55.384
on that end of the community, that’s my earliest memory was where they built the house, closer to Sulphur.

134
00:08:55.384 --> 00:08:57.849
There was wooded area all around us.

135
00:08:57.849 --> 00:09:00.314
It was our house, my grandfather’s house.

136
00:09:00.314 --> 00:09:04.189
And then we had the Townsend lived . . . and then Mrs. Davis,

137
00:09:04.189 --> 00:09:08.767
Ms. Christine, we called her, she lived right next door to my grandfather.

138
00:09:08.767 --> 00:09:14.403
Actually she was the one who was selling the land to my dad and my grandfather.

139
00:09:14.403 --> 00:09:17.925
And so she lived right next door to my grandfather,

140
00:09:17.925 --> 00:09:23.560
and then down from her were the Townsend family, the Braxton, [?] and Laurie Braxton’s family was.

141
00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:29.195
And then I don't remember when and how my uncle moved into that same, smaller community.

142
00:09:29.195 --> 00:09:31.660
But he and his family were back.

143
00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:39.409
Earliest memories was a lot of woods, a lot of playing in the woods, a lot of the traditional little kids stuff:

144
00:09:39.409 --> 00:09:47.157
the berries, picking the blackberries, and finding the fruit trees, and going up to the railroad tracks when we weren't supposed to.

145
00:09:47.157 --> 00:09:50.679
And then Mr. Perkins was still alive at the time.

146
00:09:50.679 --> 00:09:59.484
I can remember we would be back there, there was trails that would go back there, and we would end up at Mr. Perkins’ house.

147
00:09:59.484 --> 00:10:10.050
And the big memory I have with Mr. Perkins is seeing his big house with the front porch and a pear tree. [laughs] We would go back and get pears.

148
00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:14.629
<c.p>Going back to the uncle and his family, he had a large family.

149
00:10:14.629 --> 00:10:22.377
When my mom would go to work and my dad and mom would go to work, sometimes his wife would keep us.

150
00:10:22.377 --> 00:10:24.843
She would keep us during the daytime.

151
00:10:24.843 --> 00:10:34.000
So we spent a lot of time with the cousins back there, loving and fighting and doing bad things, what the other kids would do.

152
00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:38.947
And then we would, then, of course, like I said, then she would pick us up.

153
00:10:38.947 --> 00:10:42.658
This was probably even before my grandfather moved, I can remember that.

154
00:10:42.658 --> 00:10:48.842
So it would have been between ’50 and . . . because I think he moved around ‘54, something like that, ‘53, ‘54.

155
00:10:48.842 --> 00:10:51.934
But my Aunt Eva took care of us a lot.

156
00:10:51.934 --> 00:10:56.572
And like I said, there was a lot of . . . With the property we had available,

157
00:10:56.572 --> 00:10:59.664
the one thing my parents . . . It was a thing, too.

158
00:10:59.664 --> 00:11:04.303
My joke is, we probably had attention deficit in the family for a long time.

159
00:11:04.303 --> 00:11:08.322
But when my parents got through with a structured lifestyle of staying focused,

160
00:11:08.322 --> 00:11:14.197
get your tasks done or else, somehow we were able to overcome this how to stay focused on stuff.

161
00:11:14.197 --> 00:11:16.671
A lot of chores as we got older.

162
00:11:16.671 --> 00:11:21.000
When they would leave for work, there was definitely do this, do that.

163
00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:23.280
<v DRAGOON>What kinds of chores did you do?

164
00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:23.931
<v LATOUR-PITRE>House cleaning.

165
00:11:23.931 --> 00:11:27.839
Making sure everything was cleaned up in the house and the yard.

166
00:11:27.839 --> 00:11:31.096
My oldest brother was the only boy at the time.

167
00:11:31.096 --> 00:11:32.398
But general property upkeep.

168
00:11:32.398 --> 00:11:33.375
Doing some laundry.

169
00:11:33.375 --> 00:11:35.330
At that age, whatever we could.

170
00:11:35.330 --> 00:11:42.820
But progressively, they grew up with the concept of by the time you’re six or seven years old, you’re working, you’re doing something.

171
00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:50.962
So by the time we were seven or eight or nine years old, we were doing something and we were capable of doing the laundry.

172
00:11:50.962 --> 00:11:55.847
They didn’t give us things that they figured we couldn’t handle or shouldn’t be doing.

173
00:11:55.847 --> 00:12:01.057
But it was simple chores that had to be done by the time they got back.

174
00:12:01.057 --> 00:12:01.383
So

175
00:12:01.383 --> 00:12:07.245
<c.p>typically for kids, sometimes we’d spend most of the day fighting and playing, finding other things to do.

176
00:12:07.245 --> 00:12:15.713
And then one of the family jokes as well is my dad had, by then, had a little car that had a sun visor on it.

177
00:12:15.713 --> 00:12:20.923
And somehow, because it was wooded, you couldn’t see the road the way you can now.

178
00:12:20.923 --> 00:12:28.739
But you could hear this car coming and the sound, the air, I guess, through the visor or something, it made a whistling sound.

179
00:12:28.739 --> 00:12:31.670
And boy, the whistling sound, was, it was scurrying.

180
00:12:31.670 --> 00:12:37.858
It was like making sure whatever was not finished was trying to get done in the last thirty seconds.

181
00:12:37.858 --> 00:12:43.720
Because at that time, a lot of times, he would even probably have to pick up my mom.

182
00:12:43.720 --> 00:12:46.000
They just had the one car.

183
00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:53.641
I can remember as kids we would line up all the rocking chairs in the living room and we’d play train and the conductor.

184
00:12:53.641 --> 00:12:57.780
We would line up . . . That was one of the things we would do.

185
00:12:57.780 --> 00:13:01.282
And typical for older siblings, he was the man in charge.

186
00:13:01.282 --> 00:13:06.376
So if he decided we were going to play a certain game, that’s what everybody did.

187
00:13:06.376 --> 00:13:15.927
So our joke with the family again was I was the one that always decided no. [laughs] It was interesting because we grew up with this sibling rivalry, I guess.

188
00:13:15.927 --> 00:13:19.747
It was like, you’re not the boss of me kind of thing.

189
00:13:19.747 --> 00:13:22.931
So I would fight back with him all the time.

190
00:13:22.931 --> 00:13:31.208
And to this day, the other day, his wife was telling me that now he’s had a lot of strokes and he’s kind of like handicapped.

191
00:13:31.208 --> 00:13:35.347
But she said, “Don’t you want to go to the other sister’s house?”

192
00:13:35.347 --> 00:13:37.257
“No,” he said, “she don’t argue.

193
00:13:37.257 --> 00:13:40.441
I like to go to Margaret’s house because she argues.

194
00:13:40.441 --> 00:13:42.669
She’ll just . . . she just agreed on everything.”

195
00:13:42.669 --> 00:13:49.355
So to this day we still, to the extent that we can, go back and forth about the least little issue.

196
00:13:49.355 --> 00:13:55.404
So needless to say a lot of times as kids he’d say, “Let’s do this,” or, “Let’s play that.”

197
00:13:55.404 --> 00:14:00.180
And we had to go through a little fight before it was agreed on what.

198
00:14:00.180 --> 00:14:04.000
So we spent a lot of time playing hard, working hard.

199
00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:07.250
When my parents were home, they were strict disciplinarians.

200
00:14:07.250 --> 00:14:11.222
Tell everybody, I don't know, maybe I am a violent person.

201
00:14:11.222 --> 00:14:12.667
But I got spankings.

202
00:14:12.667 --> 00:14:14.472
My parents never liked . . . We

203
00:14:14.472 --> 00:14:17.722
<c.p>distinguished between what’s a spanking and what’s a beating.

204
00:14:17.722 --> 00:14:19.528
They . . . We never got beatings.

205
00:14:19.528 --> 00:14:23.139
It was like, they didn’t just go ballistic on us.

206
00:14:23.139 --> 00:14:30.361
But we knew that there were certain things that we had to meet, or else there were some serious consequence.

207
00:14:30.361 --> 00:14:36.500
So, and again, the joke is like, I don't know why you could never get that, Margaret.

208
00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:39.750
He was usually the one who was in trouble.

209
00:14:39.750 --> 00:14:41.194
So there was discipline.

210
00:14:41.194 --> 00:14:43.361
Mom was a very devout Catholic.

211
00:14:43.361 --> 00:14:45.528
There was a lot of prayer,

212
00:14:45.528 --> 00:14:56.000
a lot of those waking up at 5:30 in the morning to go to six o’clock mass because it was the first Friday or the first Saturday. [laughs]

213
00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:58.083
<v DRAGOON>Where did you go to church?

214
00:14:58.083 --> 00:15:02.944
<v LATOUR-PITRE>We went to Saint Peter Claver, I think, in Sulphur for a long time.

215
00:15:02.944 --> 00:15:08.153
And early teens or so we started going to Saint John Bosco in Westlake [Louisiana].

216
00:15:08.153 --> 00:15:15.792
So there was a lot of times to get the jobs done, to do the work, do what you have to do.

217
00:15:15.792 --> 00:15:19.611
But then you also had a lot of time to play.

218
00:15:19.611 --> 00:15:26.556
I like to think of my family as there was a really good balance between what’s work and what’s play.

219
00:15:26.556 --> 00:15:29.333
They both had a good sense of humor.

220
00:15:29.333 --> 00:15:31.069
They would tease each other.

221
00:15:31.069 --> 00:15:37.319
My dad would literally get out in the yard and race with us and played softball with us.

222
00:15:37.319 --> 00:15:45.306
And by that time, my grandfather and his family was there and their children were like our ages and a little bit older.

223
00:15:45.306 --> 00:15:48.083
So everybody would get out in the yard.

224
00:15:48.083 --> 00:15:52.944
And then he and his sibling would start big fights about who was cheating.

225
00:15:52.944 --> 00:15:55.375
So it was a lot of fun.

226
00:15:55.375 --> 00:15:57.458
It was . . . Basically, that was it.

227
00:15:57.458 --> 00:16:00.931
I can remember a lot of fun in the backyard.

228
00:16:00.931 --> 00:16:05.792
But I can also remember very strict guidelines about what needed to be done.

229
00:16:05.792 --> 00:16:08.569
<v DRAGOON>Was your mother doing most of the discipline?

230
00:16:08.569 --> 00:16:11.000
Or did they share that responsibility?

231
00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:11.891
<v LATOUR-PITRE>No, they shared.

232
00:16:11.891 --> 00:16:16.644
She’d tell you, “Wait until your father got home.” [laughs] And it wasn’t a put off.

233
00:16:16.644 --> 00:16:22.584
If she felt that it was necessary for her to take care of the problem at the moment, she did.

234
00:16:22.584 --> 00:16:27.634
That’s the other thing I like about when I remember them, I could hear them discussing sometimes.

235
00:16:27.634 --> 00:16:32.089
In the morning as you wake up, you could hear them in their bed, talking.

236
00:16:32.089 --> 00:16:37.139
And they would get together and they would discuss their issues and about what was going on.

237
00:16:37.139 --> 00:16:41.891
One of the things I liked about both of them was, “What did your mother say?”

238
00:16:41.891 --> 00:16:45.158
Today it’s just so . . . and kids know how to do it.

239
00:16:45.158 --> 00:16:46.347
Like, “Can I go?”

240
00:16:46.347 --> 00:16:47.238
It was nothing.

241
00:16:47.238 --> 00:16:52.287
I don’t care how long it had been before she’d have gotten home, “Not until your mother.

242
00:16:52.287 --> 00:16:53.772
What did your mother say?”

243
00:16:53.772 --> 00:16:59.119
And until he got confirmation from my mom that it was okay, he didn’t just say, “Well, okay.

244
00:16:59.119 --> 00:17:00.901
If she says okay, it’s alright.”

245
00:17:00.901 --> 00:17:02.980
It was like, no. He made sure.

246
00:17:02.980 --> 00:17:06.545
You couldn’t just tell him that and he would buy into that.

247
00:17:06.545 --> 00:17:07.733
And same with her.

248
00:17:07.733 --> 00:17:11.000
If she told you, “Wait till your father gets home.”

249
00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:14.341
So they collaborated, and they talked and they shared.

250
00:17:14.341 --> 00:17:16.569
And they came to mutual decisions.

251
00:17:16.569 --> 00:17:22.509
Whether they had . . . Because the discussions was probably because they didn’t both agree on the decision.

252
00:17:22.509 --> 00:17:29.934
But they would come to a mutual decision that you knew you’re not going to play them against each other,

253
00:17:29.934 --> 00:17:34.389
because this is where it’s going to be and where it’s going.

254
00:17:34.389 --> 00:17:38.102
So I think that was good for us as well.

255
00:17:38.102 --> 00:17:42.557
It made it easy. [laughs] We kind of know what to expect.

256
00:17:42.557 --> 00:17:47.754
We didn’t have to remember who we’re blowing smoke at a time, next time.

257
00:17:47.754 --> 00:17:49.611
So they had strict guidelines.

258
00:17:49.611 --> 00:17:54.066
Mom was probably the more lenient in terms of trying new things.

259
00:17:54.066 --> 00:18:00.006
Dad was more like, if it’s working, why do we have to change it, sort of.

260
00:18:00.006 --> 00:18:02.976
But she was the one who would say,

261
00:18:02.976 --> 00:18:07.431
<c.p>“Well, Raymond,” like allowance for opportunities or for something, some new experiences.

262
00:18:07.431 --> 00:18:09.287
Then again, they would discuss.

263
00:18:09.287 --> 00:18:13.000
And then he would agree or it didn’t happen.

264
00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:15.735
<v DRAGOON>Did your family have a garden at the house?

265
00:18:15.735 --> 00:18:16.343
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Oh, yes.

266
00:18:16.343 --> 00:18:16.647
Yes.

267
00:18:16.647 --> 00:18:18.775
Oh, pretty much most of the time.

268
00:18:18.775 --> 00:18:20.598
That was another memory I have.

269
00:18:20.598 --> 00:18:24.549
It wasn’t so big that we had a lot of work to do.

270
00:18:24.549 --> 00:18:26.980
But we would help with tending the garden.

271
00:18:26.980 --> 00:18:30.324
The memories of the fresh produce right out of the garden.

272
00:18:30.324 --> 00:18:31.235
He had gardens.

273
00:18:31.235 --> 00:18:33.667
There was usually a chicken or a pig.

274
00:18:33.667 --> 00:18:35.186
One time there was rabbits.

275
00:18:35.186 --> 00:18:36.402
He was raising rabbits.

276
00:18:36.402 --> 00:18:38.529
And these were all for food purposes.

277
00:18:38.529 --> 00:18:43.392
And then every now and again . . . like we didn’t think of them in terms of pets.

278
00:18:43.392 --> 00:18:45.520
It was like, this is for food.

279
00:18:45.520 --> 00:18:47.039
So we did have that.

280
00:18:47.039 --> 00:18:51.902
And when I said yard works and chores, these would all be the kinds of things:

281
00:18:51.902 --> 00:18:56.157
take care of the animals, take care of the garden, help with the yard.

282
00:18:56.157 --> 00:19:02.539
Everybody had . . . if it was a yard day, everybody had to get out, because it was a little over an acre.

283
00:19:02.539 --> 00:19:07.098
He would take care of my grandfather’s, too, so there were bricks to be moved.

284
00:19:07.098 --> 00:19:15.000
And in the process of getting us to do all these chores, he always had a technique or a way that he would show us.

285
00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:18.547
My father only had a fourth grade . . . barely a fourth grade education,

286
00:19:18.547 --> 00:19:28.300
before the owner of . . . the sharecropper—my grandfather was a sharecropper when he was a kid—decided he was needed to do the work more than it was necessary for him to go to school.

287
00:19:28.300 --> 00:19:30.665
So he barely had a fourth grade education.

288
00:19:30.665 --> 00:19:35.690
But the family joke is that if he’d been educated, he’d probably have been a rocket scientist,

289
00:19:35.690 --> 00:19:39.532
because he had an ability to figure things out and to assess things.

290
00:19:39.532 --> 00:19:41.601
And then if he would learn things,

291
00:19:41.601 --> 00:19:50.468
he left himself . . . That was one thing he did try to do was leave himself open to hear stuff that he could use as knowledge because he couldn’t read about

292
00:19:50.468 --> 00:19:50.764
<c.p>it.

293
00:19:50.764 --> 00:19:54.606
When we got older, we noticed he watched the news faithfully every day.

294
00:19:54.606 --> 00:19:59.631
He wanted to see what was going on with the news so he could talk about it.

295
00:19:59.631 --> 00:20:03.473
There were times when he did talk about not being able to read,

296
00:20:03.473 --> 00:20:09.975
when he considered maybe trying to go to school and learn how to read and just decided it was just too much.

297
00:20:09.975 --> 00:20:10.567
He stopped.

298
00:20:10.567 --> 00:20:15.000
But you could see he had the thought that he wished he could read.

299
00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:20.231
But he would either pick up techniques from his jobs or . . . and he would bring them home.

300
00:20:20.231 --> 00:20:24.231
So there was always a way of showing you how to do it.

301
00:20:24.231 --> 00:20:27.615
Inside family joke is— even if it’s wrong, do it right.

302
00:20:27.615 --> 00:20:31.308
Because he was really big on doing the job the right way.

303
00:20:31.308 --> 00:20:33.462
And if you don’t know, ask somebody.

304
00:20:33.462 --> 00:20:37.462
And that’s how he would handle the task he would give to us.

305
00:20:37.462 --> 00:20:39.308
“Go get me a three-penny nail.”

306
00:20:39.308 --> 00:20:40.538
“What’s a three-penny nail?”

307
00:20:40.538 --> 00:20:42.385
And a Philips screwdriver or something.

308
00:20:42.385 --> 00:20:49.154
By the time you got through with him, you knew what a three-penny nail is and you knew what a screwdriver is.

309
00:20:49.154 --> 00:20:56.231
And then he would come back and say, “Well, if you didn’t know what you was looking for, why didn’t you ask me?”

310
00:20:56.231 --> 00:21:02.077
Like, “Why did you just go off and try to find something you don’t know what you’re looking for?”

311
00:21:02.077 --> 00:21:06.385
So it was a technique of teaching that applies to our life even now.

312
00:21:06.385 --> 00:21:11.923
I mean, it was such a valuable lesson for us to learn how to do a job right,

313
00:21:11.923 --> 00:21:16.231
how to ask the right questions and make sure that you’re doing it right.

314
00:21:16.231 --> 00:21:20.231
So that was . . . And both of them are like that, but especially him.

315
00:21:20.231 --> 00:21:27.000
So we always . . . As we got older, we could see how wise he was in spite of not having the education.

316
00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:30.063
And again, Mom had the eighth grade education.

317
00:21:30.063 --> 00:21:35.423
She had gone as far as she could go without going to high school.

318
00:21:35.423 --> 00:21:37.721
But she was the inquisitive one.

319
00:21:37.721 --> 00:21:44.995
And because she could read, she was the one who learned the table etiquettes, and how to do things,

320
00:21:44.995 --> 00:21:52.653
like the refined kind of things, I guess, the one who wanted to climb the mountains because of her curiosity.

321
00:21:52.653 --> 00:21:54.185
She wanted to see.

322
00:21:54.185 --> 00:21:55.716
She wanted to know.

323
00:21:55.716 --> 00:21:59.928
So she brought that to us, curiosity, too, of learning more

324
00:21:59.928 --> 00:22:00.311
<c.p>stuff.

325
00:22:00.311 --> 00:22:01.077
Try things.

326
00:22:01.077 --> 00:22:02.225
Do something different.

327
00:22:02.225 --> 00:22:04.523
So it was a good balance.

328
00:22:04.523 --> 00:22:08.734
Again, her work ethics was about as strong as my dad’s.

329
00:22:08.734 --> 00:22:11.797
They was both . . . We laugh about that, too.

330
00:22:11.797 --> 00:22:13.329
Keep the house clean.

331
00:22:13.329 --> 00:22:16.775
When you walk in her house, you smell Clorox.

332
00:22:16.775 --> 00:22:23.284
We would play in the yard all day, especially in the summer times, and it was hot.

333
00:22:23.284 --> 00:22:28.644
Bath time, the bath water was reeking of Clorox. [laughs] Never had skin infections.

334
00:22:28.644 --> 00:22:29.793
Never had chiggers.

335
00:22:29.793 --> 00:22:31.324
And check for ticks.

336
00:22:31.324 --> 00:22:38.599
But I mean, it was like being outside, she would just make sure that we were good and clean.

337
00:22:38.599 --> 00:22:43.577
And of course we didn’t have the indoor plumbing until sometime later on.

338
00:22:43.577 --> 00:22:52.000
So they would make the usual bathtub that everybody would use, with the outhouse way in the back in the yard.

339
00:22:52.000 --> 00:22:53.658
<v DRAGOON>Where was the bathtub?

340
00:22:53.658 --> 00:23:01.117
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Well at first, when we were younger, it was set up in the house, in the kitchen area.

341
00:23:01.117 --> 00:23:03.604
She would set up the bath.

342
00:23:03.604 --> 00:23:06.505
Or maybe in one of the bedrooms.

343
00:23:06.505 --> 00:23:14.378
But then as time progressed, again, a lot of this, I guess happened between ‘48 and ’55, I suppose.

344
00:23:14.378 --> 00:23:21.423
He built a garage with a laundry room—the wash house, we called it—and put the washing machine.

345
00:23:21.423 --> 00:23:24.739
So the bath would happen in there sometimes.

346
00:23:24.739 --> 00:23:30.126
So like I said, but there was a lot of baths in there.

347
00:23:30.126 --> 00:23:33.441
And then we finally progressed to indoor plumbing.

348
00:23:33.441 --> 00:23:38.000
<v DRAGOON>What year did you say you had the indoor plumbing?

349
00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:38.768
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Let’s see.

350
00:23:38.768 --> 00:23:41.071
Now this is totally a guess.

351
00:23:41.071 --> 00:23:46.062
Not when Pat born, because I remember bathing him in a kitchen sink.

352
00:23:46.062 --> 00:23:53.741
So it was after ‘56 when the indoor . . . because when he built the bathroom . . . yeah, because we had the well.

353
00:23:53.741 --> 00:23:55.661
Yeah, it would have been

354
00:23:55.661 --> 00:23:59.116
<c.p>like between ‘56 and maybe ‘58, during that timeframe.

355
00:23:59.116 --> 00:24:08.714
Because I can remember when Patrick was born, where the kitchen was, and we were still bathing him as a baby in that kitchen sink.

356
00:24:08.714 --> 00:24:12.170
And then after that, a little while after that,

357
00:24:12.170 --> 00:24:19.464
that kitchen got turned into a bedroom with a bathroom and that’s when he remodeled some of the house.

358
00:24:19.464 --> 00:24:22.920
Because that was the other thing with the house.

359
00:24:22.920 --> 00:24:29.446
I tell people that every stick, board and nail on this house, my dad put it here.

360
00:24:29.446 --> 00:24:39.429
He started with a four-room house and then he added it and then, and as his family was growing, he added the bathroom and more bedrooms.

361
00:24:39.429 --> 00:24:46.723
And then the final section he added, I guess, five years before he died, six years before he died.

362
00:24:46.723 --> 00:24:50.946
So it was around, like I said, between ‘56 and ‘58.

363
00:24:50.946 --> 00:24:56.705
And one of my siblings could probably tell you more exactly what year it was,

364
00:24:56.705 --> 00:25:04.000
but it was around that timeframe from my memory right now, he finally converted and we had inside.

365
00:25:04.000 --> 00:25:06.502
<v DRAGOON>What year did he pass away?

366
00:25:06.502 --> 00:25:08.587
<v LATOUR-PITRE>My granddaughter’s eleven this year.

367
00:25:08.587 --> 00:25:09.421
So 2004.

368
00:25:09.421 --> 00:25:09.838
Yeah.

369
00:25:09.838 --> 00:25:11.506
And Mom died 2005.

370
00:25:11.506 --> 00:25:11.923
Yeah.

371
00:25:11.923 --> 00:25:19.430
<v DRAGOON>If I go back a little bit, can you tell me a little bit more about your garden?

372
00:25:19.430 --> 00:25:22.349
About what kinds of vegetables and fruits . . .

373
00:25:22.349 --> 00:25:23.600
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Tomatoes and cucumbers.

374
00:25:23.600 --> 00:25:25.685
He tried watermelons one time.

375
00:25:25.685 --> 00:25:26.936
Green beans, okra.

376
00:25:26.936 --> 00:25:34.026
Turns out I was allergic to the okra leaves, so I got out of cutting the okra.

377
00:25:34.026 --> 00:25:38.196
I wasn’t … And those are the kind of things, too.

378
00:25:38.196 --> 00:25:41.949
We had to go pick the produce and stuff.

379
00:25:41.949 --> 00:25:44.868
Those are some of the more staples.

380
00:25:44.868 --> 00:25:46.953
And maybe he tried potatoes.

381
00:25:46.953 --> 00:25:50.289
But it wasn’t . . . Like I say, it wasn’t

382
00:25:50.289 --> 00:25:51.957
<c.p>like a grand garden.

383
00:25:51.957 --> 00:26:02.383
It was just big enough to kind of feed the family and so those were some of the kind of things that I can remember.

384
00:26:02.383 --> 00:26:05.719
<v DRAGOON>Did you share, sell or trade with neighbors?

385
00:26:05.719 --> 00:26:13.226
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Especially because, like I said, our biggest, people we knew most and played with the most was family.

386
00:26:13.226 --> 00:26:13.643
Definitely.

387
00:26:13.643 --> 00:26:14.060
Always.

388
00:26:14.060 --> 00:26:17.396
Yeah, the families would always share whatever . . . Everybody.

389
00:26:17.396 --> 00:26:24.902
I can remember sometimes some of my relatives would, my mother’s sisters or somebody would get the commodities.

390
00:26:24.902 --> 00:26:33.660
When they ended up with more stuff than they could use, then they would share with her and stuff like that.

391
00:26:33.660 --> 00:26:36.996
Because they weren’t . . . my parents wasn’t getting it.

392
00:26:36.996 --> 00:26:42.000
But we drank commodity milk, powdered milk, a few times. [laughs]

393
00:26:42.000 --> 00:26:42.873
<v DRAGOON>What is that?

394
00:26:42.873 --> 00:26:43.455
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Powdered milk.

395
00:26:43.455 --> 00:26:44.328
<v DRAGOON>Oh, powdered milk.

396
00:26:44.328 --> 00:26:47.238
<v LATOUR-PITRE>We had powdered milk in the house a few times.

397
00:26:47.238 --> 00:26:51.604
I hate powdered milk. [laughs] So yeah, there was a lot of sharing of childcare.

398
00:26:51.604 --> 00:26:57.424
And like I said, again, with her brother’s family, because Mrs. Davis, she was kind of like the neighborhood watch.

399
00:26:57.424 --> 00:27:00.043
She’d keep an eye on things and could report.

400
00:27:00.043 --> 00:27:03.245
That was another thing, too, that we like about the neighborhood.

401
00:27:03.245 --> 00:27:07.028
If you were in the neighborhood, and the neighbors saw you doing something . . .

402
00:27:07.028 --> 00:27:09.938
<c.p>This was a typical story most people grew up with.

403
00:27:09.938 --> 00:27:14.303
If the neighbors saw you doing something that wasn’t right, your parents knew about it.

404
00:27:14.303 --> 00:27:17.505
<v DRAGOON>And Ms. Davis was one of the ones who’d report back?

405
00:27:17.505 --> 00:27:19.542
<v LATOUR-PITRE>She was one of the neighborhood watch.

406
00:27:19.542 --> 00:27:25.653
When we first got our phone, again, I’m not sure what year, I guess early ‘50s, it was a party line.

407
00:27:25.653 --> 00:27:32.056
And we were in a party line with Ms. Davis. [laughs] So you had to be careful how you used the phone,

408
00:27:32.056 --> 00:27:37.876
especially when the parents were gone at work during the daytime and you decide you want to make prank calls.

409
00:27:37.876 --> 00:27:39.913
Yeah, we didn’t do that a lot.

410
00:27:39.913 --> 00:27:43.697
Ms. Davis could . . . she was always there to listen and check on us.

411
00:27:43.697 --> 00:27:47.771
And sometimes she would pick up the phone, “Can you get off the phone?

412
00:27:47.771 --> 00:27:49.808
I need to make a phone call.”

413
00:27:49.808 --> 00:27:52.427
Again, the kids weren’t allowed a big phone user.

414
00:27:52.427 --> 00:27:58.830
But as children are, you did try to play with the phone a little bit, to find some reason to use it.

415
00:27:58.830 --> 00:28:02.904
But our closest relationships were with a cousin who was just within walking distance.

416
00:28:02.904 --> 00:28:08.433
We would just walk up the road and then go back into the woods and we could be there,

417
00:28:08.433 --> 00:28:11.053
spend some time with them, and sharing with them.

418
00:28:11.053 --> 00:28:16.000
And then she had another sister who got married and moved into the Mossville proper area.

419
00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:17.061
<v DRAGOON>Who did?

420
00:28:17.061 --> 00:28:18.652
<v LATOUR-PITRE>My mother’s sister.

421
00:28:18.652 --> 00:28:28.200
She had another sister, [Dajos?], I don't know if you had a chance to talk to her family.

422
00:28:28.200 --> 00:28:31.383
Or [Darjean?], some people call them.

423
00:28:31.383 --> 00:28:43.583
So that we would invite in, everybody pretty much had a car and we would run back and forth and visit with them.

424
00:28:43.583 --> 00:28:48.887
<c.p>Oh, and she did have another sister, actually, who moved.

425
00:28:48.887 --> 00:28:52.600
That was the [Datrus?], the [Datrus?] family.

426
00:28:52.600 --> 00:28:57.904
They moved and left and went to California early on.

427
00:28:57.904 --> 00:29:02.148
Again, ’58, maybe, about ’58, they were gone.

428
00:29:02.148 --> 00:29:06.391
They went to California, they moved to California.

429
00:29:06.391 --> 00:29:15.409
We ended up with quite a few relatives around that we visited with a lot, shared with.

430
00:29:15.409 --> 00:29:15.939
<v PETE>Hello.

431
00:29:15.939 --> 00:29:17.000
<v DRAGOON>Hi.

432
00:29:17.000 --> 00:29:18.203
<v LATOUR-PITRE>That’s my husband Pete.

433
00:29:18.203 --> 00:29:22.712
And the other thing that we did, too, as entertainment in the household, for sure.

434
00:29:22.712 --> 00:29:28.124
I don't know if you heard that, I think Kim had mentioned that my father was a musician.

435
00:29:28.124 --> 00:29:28.425
<v DRAGOON>Yes.

436
00:29:28.425 --> 00:29:34.137
<v LATOUR-PITRE>And that was another one of those things that I’d forgot to mention earlier about the playing and dancing.

437
00:29:34.137 --> 00:29:36.843
We work hard, we played hard, we danced hard.

438
00:29:36.843 --> 00:29:41.353
He would start playing and it was just, at the time, the five of us.

439
00:29:41.353 --> 00:29:43.458
He and my mom, and my sister,

440
00:29:43.458 --> 00:29:50.072
and my brother and I. Mom would grab us—she’s the one that taught us to dance—and we’d do waltz, we’d do two-steps.

441
00:29:50.072 --> 00:29:51.275
He played the music.

442
00:29:51.275 --> 00:29:53.680
One of us would rub a rubbing board.

443
00:29:53.680 --> 00:29:56.085
He showed us how to use the spoons.

444
00:29:56.085 --> 00:29:58.490
So we had a little band thing going.

445
00:29:58.490 --> 00:30:03.000
We spent a lot of time just making music in the household, just us.

446
00:30:03.000 --> 00:30:06.471
<v DRAGOON>Did he teach you how to make music, too?

447
00:30:06.471 --> 00:30:08.786
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Nobody learned to play the accordion.

448
00:30:08.786 --> 00:30:13.414
I guess at best we learned to really appreciate music in general.

449
00:30:13.414 --> 00:30:22.671
Because Mom, again, was one of these people where the music was fine but she was curious about other kinds of music as well.

450
00:30:22.671 --> 00:30:23.829
<v DRAGOON>Did she play?

451
00:30:23.829 --> 00:30:25.757
<v LATOUR-PITRE>No, she wasn’t a musician.

452
00:30:25.757 --> 00:30:30.000
<v DRAGOON>How did your dad learn how to play the accordion?

453
00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:32.318
<v LATOUR-PITRE>The term they use, play by ear.

454
00:30:32.318 --> 00:30:34.968
His father and his grandfather played the accordion.

455
00:30:34.968 --> 00:30:42.917
And as a child, the story goes, as he would hear his father playing, he would look at him and he would watch him.

456
00:30:42.917 --> 00:30:46.561
And his father didn’t realize that he was picking it up.

457
00:30:46.561 --> 00:30:48.879
And always threatened, “Don’t play my accordion.

458
00:30:48.879 --> 00:30:49.873
Leave it alone.”

459
00:30:49.873 --> 00:30:55.503
Apparently one time his grandfather heard him, this is the story I heard, heard my dad play.

460
00:30:55.503 --> 00:31:03.121
And when his father started chastising him about playing with the accordion, his grandfather was the one who told him, “Leave him alone.

461
00:31:03.121 --> 00:31:04.115
Let him play.

462
00:31:04.115 --> 00:31:05.108
He plays well.”

463
00:31:05.108 --> 00:31:06.433
And he started playing.

464
00:31:06.433 --> 00:31:15.045
So I guess as a teenager, seventeen, eighteen, nineteen, you’re a young man, his sister told me one night when she was young, he was playing.

465
00:31:15.045 --> 00:31:18.688
So by seventeen, eighteen, I guess, he was playing house dances.

466
00:31:18.688 --> 00:31:21.669
Of course, back then, it was all house dances.

467
00:31:21.669 --> 00:31:25.312
<c.p>Just to go back, a story on my dad as well,

468
00:31:25.312 --> 00:31:31.936
when his mom died when he was nine years old, he had a sister and two brothers at the time.

469
00:31:31.936 --> 00:31:34.255
But he was about nine years old.

470
00:31:34.255 --> 00:31:37.567
The sister was six, I guess, or something like that.

471
00:31:37.567 --> 00:31:42.535
But anyway, the maternal grandparents decided the father couldn’t take care of the smaller children.

472
00:31:42.535 --> 00:31:45.516
The youngest brother was an infant when she died.

473
00:31:45.516 --> 00:31:49.822
So the last three children moved away to live with the maternal grandparents.

474
00:31:49.822 --> 00:31:54.790
And my dad stayed with his dad because, again, he’s nine years old by then.

475
00:31:54.790 --> 00:31:55.452
They’re working.

476
00:31:55.452 --> 00:32:02.076
So he was able to take care of himself and to be able to help with work around the house.

477
00:32:02.076 --> 00:32:04.726
So my grandfather kept him there with him.

478
00:32:04.726 --> 00:32:08.701
So he and his siblings kind of grew up kind of apart.

479
00:32:08.701 --> 00:32:14.000
They would visit with each other, but it wasn’t like growing up with your siblings.

480
00:32:14.000 --> 00:32:23.344
I remember when she was telling me this story about being at this house dance as she started to get older and she saw him playing,

481
00:32:23.344 --> 00:32:31.609
how it hit her that this was her oldest brother and how well he was playing and how she was fascinated with it.

482
00:32:31.609 --> 00:32:35.562
And she said . . . He would acknowledge her and he came over.

483
00:32:35.562 --> 00:32:40.594
But they didn’t really have a relationship going, because they hadn’t grown up together.

484
00:32:40.594 --> 00:32:42.391
But they acknowledged each other.

485
00:32:42.391 --> 00:32:47.422
So it was after he married Mom that they reestablished a relationship, the siblings.

486
00:32:47.422 --> 00:32:53.172
And like I said, they very much grew to be a part of each other’s lives.

487
00:32:53.172 --> 00:33:00.000
But the memories of the house dances we had on a smaller scale were some really good memories.

488
00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:02.727
I did take a stab at being a piano player.

489
00:33:02.727 --> 00:33:05.455
This was one of these priceless comments that he made.

490
00:33:05.455 --> 00:33:10.909
Mom had a cousin all the way in Houston [Texas] that had a piano she wanted to get rid of.

491
00:33:10.909 --> 00:33:13.636
He and Mom . . . He had a truck, an old truck.

492
00:33:13.636 --> 00:33:20.727
Drove it all the way to Houston, loaded up the piano, brought it all the way back, because she wanted her children to take piano lessons.

493
00:33:20.727 --> 00:33:21.273
I volunteered.

494
00:33:21.273 --> 00:33:22.091
I’ll do it.

495
00:33:22.091 --> 00:33:25.091
In transporting the piano, a lot of the keys were damaged.

496
00:33:25.091 --> 00:33:26.727
So it was a half piano.

497
00:33:26.727 --> 00:33:29.182
But most of it was, started taking piano lessons.

498
00:33:29.182 --> 00:33:30.000
And took piano

499
00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:31.364
<c.p>lessons for five years, maybe.

500
00:33:31.364 --> 00:33:32.455
Progressing through the books.

501
00:33:32.455 --> 00:33:33.273
Doing piano lessons.

502
00:33:33.273 --> 00:33:33.818
Doing okay.

503
00:33:33.818 --> 00:33:37.364
Started getting to the phase where you don’t want to practice that much.

504
00:33:37.364 --> 00:33:42.000
One day my dad looked at me and he said, “You really want to play this thing?”

505
00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:43.091
I said, “Well, yeah.”

506
00:33:43.091 --> 00:33:47.727
When he asked you a question like that, you didn't want to say no. [laughs] “Well, yeah.”

507
00:33:47.727 --> 00:33:49.091
He says, “No, you don’t.”

508
00:33:49.091 --> 00:33:50.455
“Well, what do you mean?

509
00:33:50.455 --> 00:33:52.909
Why do you think I don’t want to play?”

510
00:33:52.909 --> 00:33:57.000
He said, “Because I can see the way you sit there and play that piano.”

511
00:33:57.000 --> 00:34:01.364
He said, “If you really felt that music and you really wanted to play that piano,”

512
00:34:01.364 --> 00:34:05.727
he says . . . And the cousin who they got it from, that’s how she played the piano.

513
00:34:05.727 --> 00:34:06.818
She worked the piano.

514
00:34:06.818 --> 00:34:09.000
I mean, she loved playing the piano.

515
00:34:09.000 --> 00:34:10.236
I never forgot that.

516
00:34:10.236 --> 00:34:13.636
It was like . . . And again, it’s one of those life lessons.

517
00:34:13.636 --> 00:34:21.055
Like if you really have a passion for something, you’re going to feel it, you’re going to know it, you’re going to demonstrate it.

518
00:34:21.055 --> 00:34:27.545
If you’re just doing something because it’s a challenge or you don’t know why you’re doing it, put down the piano.

519
00:34:27.545 --> 00:34:30.327
Learn how to appreciate good music of all kinds.

520
00:34:30.327 --> 00:34:32.491
But recognize, maybe you’re not a musician.

521
00:34:32.491 --> 00:34:38.055
The only family of my siblings who ended up with a natural music talent was the youngest brother.

522
00:34:38.055 --> 00:34:39.291
He played the drums.

523
00:34:39.291 --> 00:34:42.691
And ever since he was a little boy, little tiny kid,

524
00:34:42.691 --> 00:34:48.255
he started beating on containers and buckets and did that until Mom realized he does have a talent,

525
00:34:48.255 --> 00:34:53.818
he really likes this, and bought him a set of drums when he was eleven, twelve, maybe thirteen.

526
00:34:53.818 --> 00:35:00.000
I’ll never forget that Christmas when he walked in and they had bought a set of drums for him.

527
00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:01.446
<v DRAGOON>Does he still play?

528
00:35:01.446 --> 00:35:02.169
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Still plays.

529
00:35:02.169 --> 00:35:03.614
<v DRAGOON>What kind of music?

530
00:35:03.614 --> 00:35:10.120
<v LATOUR-PITRE>He plays . . . I think he really likes jazz kinds of music, R&amp;B kind of music, and some zydeco.

531
00:35:10.120 --> 00:35:15.542
As a matter of fact, he was indoctrinated with what was then being called zydeco.

532
00:35:15.542 --> 00:35:19.880
Because my thing with my dad and the music he played is,

533
00:35:19.880 --> 00:35:29.277
my dad never really got into the zydeco sound with the big accordion and the rhythmic beat of the . . . He stayed more on the Cajun side.

534
00:35:29.277 --> 00:35:32.169
My dad really played Cajun music a lot.

535
00:35:32.169 --> 00:35:39.759
When Felice got old enough, after he had a set of drums, fifteen, sixteen, seventeen years old, by seventeen years old,

536
00:35:39.759 --> 00:35:44.819
he started playing with my dad, because by then my dad had a band,

537
00:35:44.819 --> 00:35:49.518
a little small band that he was going around playing dances different places.

538
00:35:49.518 --> 00:35:51.687
<v DRAGOON>And what was the band called?

539
00:35:51.687 --> 00:35:53.855
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Raymond LaTour and the Sulfur Playboys.

540
00:35:53.855 --> 00:36:00.000
And I have little fliers and stuff that I’ll show you when he first started playing.

541
00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:02.358
<v DRAGOON>Did they ever play in Mossville?

542
00:36:02.358 --> 00:36:05.502
I know there’s the place called the Paradise.

543
00:36:05.502 --> 00:36:08.253
Or any other places that you remember?

544
00:36:08.253 --> 00:36:11.790
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Well, he played . . . I remember some of the cities.

545
00:36:11.790 --> 00:36:14.934
But the exact . . . Because that was another thing.

546
00:36:14.934 --> 00:36:21.223
When he would go to play, it was a him and a guy kind of thing.

547
00:36:21.223 --> 00:36:23.188
Very often, we weren’t there.

548
00:36:23.188 --> 00:36:27.904
But I think he did play at the Paradise Club one time.

549
00:36:27.904 --> 00:36:32.227
One of the other siblings can confirm that a lot better.

550
00:36:32.227 --> 00:36:34.585
Certainly Felice and Raymond would know.

551
00:36:34.585 --> 00:36:35.371
But he

552
00:36:35.371 --> 00:36:38.908
<c.p>would play like Richard, I think, in Opelousas [Louisiana].

553
00:36:38.908 --> 00:36:42.052
He played a couple of places in Opelousas.

554
00:36:42.052 --> 00:36:47.948
He played some places in . . . he went out to Texas and played for different people.

555
00:36:47.948 --> 00:36:49.520
It was like invitational.

556
00:36:49.520 --> 00:36:53.450
It was like, “Can you come and play for us?”

557
00:36:53.450 --> 00:37:02.882
So between Houston and [Rugal?], he played different places along the I-10. [It wasn’t the?] Chitlin’ Circuit. [laughs] But he played for different places.

558
00:37:02.882 --> 00:37:11.921
And between ‘58, ‘59 and maybe ‘60, until after I was married, I got married in ‘66, he was playing quite a bit.

559
00:37:11.921 --> 00:37:13.886
He had almost every weekend.

560
00:37:13.886 --> 00:37:22.140
And he actually played at the New Orleans World Fair . . . at the world fair when it was held in New Orleans.

561
00:37:22.140 --> 00:37:22.926
<v DRAGOON>Oh, wow.

562
00:37:22.926 --> 00:37:30.000
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah, he and his little group actually played there for, I think they were there a week.

563
00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:31.474
<v DRAGOON>What year was that?

564
00:37:31.474 --> 00:37:32.579
Do you know?

565
00:37:32.579 --> 00:37:33.316
<v LATOUR-PITRE>I forget.

566
00:37:33.316 --> 00:37:36.263
It’s something we can look up. [note: 1984]

567
00:37:36.263 --> 00:37:37.000
<v DRAGOON>Yeah.

568
00:37:37.000 --> 00:37:40.529
<v LATOUR-PITRE>And I had a paper where they acknowledge him playing.

569
00:37:40.529 --> 00:37:42.294
And did I save things?

570
00:37:42.294 --> 00:37:45.118
I don't know where it is right now.

571
00:37:45.118 --> 00:37:47.588
I haven’t been able to find it.

572
00:37:47.588 --> 00:37:52.529
But yeah, he played at the World’s Fair when he was in New Orleans.

573
00:37:52.529 --> 00:37:58.176
Again, I guess, whenever that, if that was in the ‘50s, ‘60s, when it was there.

574
00:37:58.176 --> 00:38:02.412
He and his group was there for a week and they would

575
00:38:02.412 --> 00:38:02.765
<c.p>play.

576
00:38:02.765 --> 00:38:05.941
And I wasn’t here; I was married and gone.

577
00:38:05.941 --> 00:38:07.706
Or in the ‘70s, maybe.

578
00:38:07.706 --> 00:38:10.529
Maybe, yeah, it was probably beyond the ‘60s.

579
00:38:10.529 --> 00:38:19.000
But anyway, so he and Felice played together for quite a while, and a couple other . . . the guys that was in the band.

580
00:38:19.000 --> 00:38:22.692
<v DRAGOON>You mentioned that your grandfather was a sharecropper?

581
00:38:22.692 --> 00:38:23.154
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yes.

582
00:38:23.154 --> 00:38:24.077
Joe was.

583
00:38:24.077 --> 00:38:25.000
<v DRAGOON>Joe?

584
00:38:25.000 --> 00:38:25.469
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

585
00:38:25.469 --> 00:38:31.099
My mother’s family owned their own land forever . . . for a long time.

586
00:38:31.099 --> 00:38:39.074
I think they acquired land after the Civil War, when they were doing land grants, I guess.

587
00:38:39.074 --> 00:38:42.827
The great-great-grandfather acquired a large parcel of land.

588
00:38:42.827 --> 00:38:49.864
My grandfather himself had like thirty-five acres in Basile, the community out there in Basile.

589
00:38:49.864 --> 00:38:52.679
So he owned his own land.

590
00:38:52.679 --> 00:38:55.025
But Joe was a sharecropper.

591
00:38:55.025 --> 00:39:00.185
And my mother’s . . . You had asked me about my grandparents earlier.

592
00:39:00.185 --> 00:39:03.938
My mother’s family was Isaac Guillory and Orelia [?].

593
00:39:03.938 --> 00:39:07.222
I have pictures of them up there.

594
00:39:07.222 --> 00:39:10.975
Like I said, he owned his own farm.

595
00:39:10.975 --> 00:39:14.259
She came from a family of twelve.

596
00:39:14.259 --> 00:39:16.605
There was actually . . . no, ten.

597
00:39:16.605 --> 00:39:18.012
There was twelve.

598
00:39:18.012 --> 00:39:21.296
A son died and a daughter died.

599
00:39:21.296 --> 00:39:25.519
The remaining siblings were five sisters and five brothers.

600
00:39:25.519 --> 00:39:31.617
So it was kind of interesting how it was a perfect balance there.

601
00:39:31.617 --> 00:39:36.309
And that’s where she grew up, on the farm there.

602
00:39:36.309 --> 00:39:40.531
<v DRAGOON>Was that close to where your father grew up?

603
00:39:40.531 --> 00:39:43.346
<v LATOUR-PITRE>It was in the general . . . Yeah.

604
00:39:43.346 --> 00:39:44.753
<v DRAGOON>It wasn’t . . . okay.

605
00:39:44.753 --> 00:39:45.222
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

606
00:39:45.222 --> 00:39:47.568
It wasn’t like right close.

607
00:39:47.568 --> 00:39:51.321
But it was close enough in that day.

608
00:39:51.321 --> 00:39:58.827
And especially when they did the house parties, the people in the community would get together.

609
00:39:58.827 --> 00:40:02.580
Or church functions, and they would get together.

610
00:40:02.580 --> 00:40:12.432
I think that’s how they met, in one of these house party functions . . . party gatherings, I think is how they met.

611
00:40:12.432 --> 00:40:20.407
And of course they had to always get the proper approval on who they could talk to.

612
00:40:20.407 --> 00:40:24.630
So he started coming over to visit with her.

613
00:40:24.630 --> 00:40:36.827
One of her jokes is how he’d ride over on this very small, thin, skinny little horse. [laughs] And my father was a small man himself.

614
00:40:36.827 --> 00:40:40.580
He was, I guess, five-five, maybe, small stature.

615
00:40:40.580 --> 00:40:43.395
We call him the little Napoleon.

616
00:40:43.395 --> 00:40:50.432
He was small but mighty. [laughs] He would take it on if it killed him.

617
00:40:50.432 --> 00:40:50.901
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

618
00:40:50.901 --> 00:40:57.000
Can you talk a little bit about your education and your siblings’?

619
00:40:57.000 --> 00:41:01.763
<v LATOUR-PITRE>I started school . . . When I started school, we were going to Sacred Heart.

620
00:41:01.763 --> 00:41:08.358
We went . . . There was a community bus that would come into the neighborhood at a certain stop point.

621
00:41:08.358 --> 00:41:12.022
My mother would have to drive out to that point.

622
00:41:12.022 --> 00:41:14.587
And we would get on the bus,

623
00:41:14.587 --> 00:41:21.548
drive into . . . the bus would take us into Lake Charles on the corner of Broad [Street] and Bilbo [Street],

624
00:41:21.548 --> 00:41:24.846
or whatever the bus stop was in that area.

625
00:41:24.846 --> 00:41:32.540
And of course my brother had already been going to Sacred Heart in Lake Charles because he was two years older.

626
00:41:32.540 --> 00:41:34.738
So he had already been going.

627
00:41:34.738 --> 00:41:40.601
So when I started school, he and I would walk from that bus stop around Mill

628
00:41:40.601 --> 00:41:47.562
<c.p>[Street], Broad and Bilbo, in that area, and walk down to Sacred Heart, which is closer to Enterprise [Boulevard].

629
00:41:47.562 --> 00:41:54.157
I was there my kindergarten year and, I guess, my first grade, or second grade, and then moved.

630
00:41:54.157 --> 00:41:58.920
At that point my mother decided . . . My sister was fixing to start school,

631
00:41:58.920 --> 00:42:03.317
so she decided we were going to go to school in Westlake.

632
00:42:03.317 --> 00:42:06.248
Westlake had an all-black elementary school for children.

633
00:42:06.248 --> 00:42:09.912
We were there until about the third grade for me.

634
00:42:09.912 --> 00:42:13.942
And then they opened Mossville and that’s where I graduated from.

635
00:42:13.942 --> 00:42:17.972
From there I went to, out of high school, after graduation,

636
00:42:17.972 --> 00:42:23.468
I went to McNeese [State University] for a little over a year . . . about a year.

637
00:42:23.468 --> 00:42:26.033
First year there, I meet my husband.

638
00:42:26.033 --> 00:42:27.132
I meet Pete.

639
00:42:27.132 --> 00:42:30.796
He decided we should fall in love and get married.

640
00:42:30.796 --> 00:42:32.628
I’m going, I don't know.

641
00:42:32.628 --> 00:42:32.994
Okay.

642
00:42:32.994 --> 00:42:36.658
Sound like a good idea. [laughs] I tease my kids.

643
00:42:36.658 --> 00:42:41.788
I say, “Trust me, at that age, it was kind of like, all right.”

644
00:42:41.788 --> 00:42:45.085
And I see it so often in the kids.

645
00:42:45.085 --> 00:42:48.017
It’s like, “What do you want to do?”

646
00:42:48.017 --> 00:42:49.116
“I don't know.”

647
00:42:49.116 --> 00:42:55.711
And when I decided to go to school, I looked around and I thought what’s my best option?

648
00:42:55.711 --> 00:43:02.306
Given the options of things that I could do, I decided no, I’m going to go to school.

649
00:43:02.306 --> 00:43:08.168
Didn’t really know why I was there or what I was going to take or anything.

650
00:43:08.168 --> 00:43:10.000
But I did start.

651
00:43:10.000 --> 00:43:17.031
And then about a year and a half after I was there, Pete proposed and we got married in ‘66.

652
00:43:17.031 --> 00:43:25.116
While I was in school, during the weekdays, we stayed with a lady here in Lake Charles who would board college girls, particularly.

653
00:43:25.116 --> 00:43:31.093
So during the week I was staying with, we called her Ms. Sophie, and go to McNeese.

654
00:43:31.093 --> 00:43:33.905
And then on weekends, I would go home.

655
00:43:33.905 --> 00:43:37.420
And then the year after I started, my sister started.

656
00:43:37.420 --> 00:43:41.990
But I think when she started we were there for like one semester.

657
00:43:41.990 --> 00:43:43.748
And then I dropped out.

658
00:43:43.748 --> 00:43:46.912
In that following year, I ended up getting married.

659
00:43:46.912 --> 00:43:50.076
So I never did go back to Ms. Sophie.

660
00:43:50.076 --> 00:43:54.646
But anyway, so a year, a year, about a year, a good year,

661
00:43:54.646 --> 00:44:02.029
after being in school a year and a half, we got married in ‘66, got married on Saturday, left on Sunday.

662
00:44:02.029 --> 00:44:02.380
Pete

663
00:44:02.380 --> 00:44:04.138
<c.p>was already graduated from McNeese.

664
00:44:04.138 --> 00:44:06.599
He was a senior when we met.

665
00:44:06.599 --> 00:44:08.356
Had joined the air force.

666
00:44:08.356 --> 00:44:13.629
Was stationed up at Wright Patterson in Ohio, Wright Patterson Air Force Base in Ohio.

667
00:44:13.629 --> 00:44:21.012
So we moved up there, he and I. He was there in the air force for three and a half years.

668
00:44:21.012 --> 00:44:23.121
Decided . . . They offered an early out.

669
00:44:23.121 --> 00:44:29.097
He came out . . . This was just when the Vietnam era was fixing to go into full fledge,

670
00:44:29.097 --> 00:44:32.261
full swing, was pretty much going, was there already.

671
00:44:32.261 --> 00:44:34.722
And decided to become a government civilian.

672
00:44:34.722 --> 00:44:36.480
So we stayed in Ohio.

673
00:44:36.480 --> 00:44:42.105
A few years after we were there, I decided I wanted to go back to school.

674
00:44:42.105 --> 00:44:43.511
So the joke is,

675
00:44:43.511 --> 00:44:51.596
I literally graduated with over 200 credit hours by the time I actually got a degree. [laughs] Because I would go in school,

676
00:44:51.596 --> 00:44:54.760
I would come out, I would go in school.

677
00:44:54.760 --> 00:45:01.088
But what happened is, I realized, it hit me that I really did want to be in school.

678
00:45:01.088 --> 00:45:03.549
I actually am taking a class now.

679
00:45:03.549 --> 00:45:05.306
I like being in school.

680
00:45:05.306 --> 00:45:10.228
But in ‘81, I got my degree in chemistry, a BS degree in chemistry.

681
00:45:10.228 --> 00:45:12.337
And then started a graduate program.

682
00:45:12.337 --> 00:45:16.907
By then I was working for Monsanto Corporation on a DOE government contract.

683
00:45:16.907 --> 00:45:19.368
We were doing weaponry research and development.

684
00:45:19.368 --> 00:45:25.696
And so I was working for Monsanto doing that kind of work and I started a master’s program.

685
00:45:25.696 --> 00:45:29.914
So from ‘81 to about ‘84, I was in a master’s program.

686
00:45:29.914 --> 00:45:32.024
And ‘85, we relocated to Virginia.

687
00:45:32.024 --> 00:45:38.000
Pete had gone there for a temporary assignment and decided, again, he wanted to stay there.

688
00:45:38.000 --> 00:45:39.728
So we relocated to Virginia.

689
00:45:39.728 --> 00:45:42.493
And that’s where we finished raising our kids.

690
00:45:42.493 --> 00:45:46.294
We adopted both of our children when we were in Ohio.

691
00:45:46.294 --> 00:45:50.096
Both of our kids are adopted, a boy and a girl.

692
00:45:50.096 --> 00:45:58.044
Now a man and a woman. [laughs] So that’s kind of the . . . And like I said, with my education, followed my mother’s pattern.

693
00:45:58.044 --> 00:46:01.846
I’d get curious about something, I’d want to know about it.

694
00:46:01.846 --> 00:46:05.993
As life goes, raising the kids and working, we both worked fulltime.

695
00:46:05.993 --> 00:46:11.868
You put it aside, but then this past year I decided I’m going to do something different.

696
00:46:11.868 --> 00:46:13.250
Like I’m semi-retired right

697
00:46:13.250 --> 00:46:13.596
<c.p>now.

698
00:46:13.596 --> 00:46:14.632
He’s fully retired.

699
00:46:14.632 --> 00:46:19.471
He was going to school until this quarter he decided, I need a break.

700
00:46:19.471 --> 00:46:22.926
But I decided to go back and take a class.

701
00:46:22.926 --> 00:46:25.000
So we like McNeese. [laughs]

702
00:46:25.000 --> 00:46:27.045
<v DRAGOON>Where did you end up graduating?

703
00:46:27.045 --> 00:46:27.727
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Wright State.

704
00:46:27.727 --> 00:46:32.159
Wright State University in Dayton . . . or Fairborn, I guess, it’s in Fairborn, Ohio.

705
00:46:32.159 --> 00:46:39.318
And I started my studies because we were right up the street from a historically black university called Central State Wilberforce.

706
00:46:39.318 --> 00:46:42.386
They were those two schools that were up there.

707
00:46:42.386 --> 00:46:44.432
So I started at Central State.

708
00:46:44.432 --> 00:46:50.909
And then when I started working for Monsanto, I transferred to Wright State because they had a better program.

709
00:46:50.909 --> 00:46:53.977
I did pretty much all my studies at night.

710
00:46:53.977 --> 00:46:55.341
They had evening classes.

711
00:46:55.341 --> 00:47:00.455
So I was able to work during the daytime and go to class at night.

712
00:47:00.455 --> 00:47:07.955
Yeah, it was . . . Like I tell my kids, as long as you have a functioning brain cell, you can go to school.

713
00:47:07.955 --> 00:47:12.045
But there are times when it’s a lot easier than other times.

714
00:47:12.045 --> 00:47:20.909
When you’ve got two kids and a fulltime job and a household to run and decide you want to get a degree, then it’s a challenge.

715
00:47:20.909 --> 00:47:21.932
It’s a challenge.

716
00:47:21.932 --> 00:47:22.955
But it worked.

717
00:47:22.955 --> 00:47:25.000
And we got through it.

718
00:47:25.000 --> 00:47:26.471
After all of that,

719
00:47:26.471 --> 00:47:34.929
I haven’t been in a lab in I don't know how many years. [laughs] About twenty-five years since I’ve been in a lab.

720
00:47:34.929 --> 00:47:40.077
But the first twenty years, a lot of my time was spent in laboratories.

721
00:47:40.077 --> 00:47:42.284
I was doing research type work.

722
00:47:42.284 --> 00:47:47.800
One of the jobs I had when I first started working in a lab, actually,

723
00:47:47.800 --> 00:47:53.316
was for Fells Institute, Fells Research Institute, in a small town called Yellow Springs, Ohio.

724
00:47:53.316 --> 00:47:55.155
That’s where we were living.

725
00:47:55.155 --> 00:47:59.200
And their project was, at the time, was the space program.

726
00:47:59.200 --> 00:48:06.187
We were evaluating samples, monkey samples, samples from sacrificed monkeys, to see the effects of weightlessness on the body.

727
00:48:06.187 --> 00:48:09.865
They were trying to determine the effects of being weightless.

728
00:48:09.865 --> 00:48:14.277
So that was one of the first projects that I worked on.

729
00:48:14.277 --> 00:48:15.381
And then when

730
00:48:15.381 --> 00:48:22.000
<c.p>I left them, that’s when I ended up working for Monsanto, doing weaponry research and development. [laughs]

731
00:48:22.000 --> 00:48:23.663
<v DRAGOON>What does that entail?

732
00:48:23.663 --> 00:48:26.158
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Basically looking at weaponry type materials.

733
00:48:26.158 --> 00:48:35.307
And then one of the things that I had to do . . . And we do an analysis of the purity, or the quality.

734
00:48:35.307 --> 00:48:37.802
That’s what I was doing specifically.

735
00:48:37.802 --> 00:48:41.960
There were other people doing different other things as well.

736
00:48:41.960 --> 00:48:51.109
But my job was to look . . . I was more like on the waste end of it or the qualifying end of it,

737
00:48:51.109 --> 00:48:57.762
where I would use . . . we were using scientific instruments to determine the purity of a powder.

738
00:48:57.762 --> 00:49:00.257
It could be radioactive, or not.

739
00:49:00.257 --> 00:49:10.653
Sometimes if you’re working on a sample and you were told it’s not radioactive and you break it and you’d bomb out a whole building,

740
00:49:10.653 --> 00:49:17.723
then you find out, oh, it was. [laughs] Like oh, okay, somebody could have told us that.

741
00:49:17.723 --> 00:49:21.465
But working a lot in glove boxes, hot boxes.

742
00:49:21.465 --> 00:49:29.782
And then one of the other jobs I had was recovering the waste so it could be processed and evaporated,

743
00:49:29.782 --> 00:49:32.693
evaporating the nuclear waste off of it.

744
00:49:32.693 --> 00:49:36.020
And then re-drum and sealed and disposed of.

745
00:49:36.020 --> 00:49:38.099
So that was another thing.

746
00:49:38.099 --> 00:49:46.000
A lot of the work required working in hot . . . in the glove boxes and monitoring with the dosimeters.

747
00:49:46.000 --> 00:49:51.676
I used to tell my families, a lot of times they were concerned, “You’re working on radioactive materials?!”

748
00:49:51.676 --> 00:49:57.667
But it’s like, fortunately I never had to work with the really bad, the other people were doing that.

749
00:49:57.667 --> 00:50:01.135
Ones that I was working with, their half-life was really quick.

750
00:50:01.135 --> 00:50:06.180
They used to tell us, “Go home and drink a few beers and flush your system.”

751
00:50:06.180 --> 00:50:09.018
It would flush pretty quickly, would deteriorate pretty quickly.

752
00:50:09.018 --> 00:50:13.748
And fortunately for me, and again, I tell them that, too, it’s a question of,

753
00:50:13.748 --> 00:50:18.793
if you know what the safety rules are and you follow the rules and you don’t

754
00:50:18.793 --> 00:50:23.523
<c.p>do things you’re not supposed to, it’s hard for you to actually have an uptake.

755
00:50:23.523 --> 00:50:26.360
Something has to happen that’s outside of your control.

756
00:50:26.360 --> 00:50:32.351
If you’re doing your job well, it’s not that easy . . . because they have a lot of precautions in place.

757
00:50:32.351 --> 00:50:38.973
And then they monitor your, and then sometimes when we had to work with the materials and [undrumming?] or opening something,

758
00:50:38.973 --> 00:50:41.180
we had to go full body suit.

759
00:50:41.180 --> 00:50:46.541
Where it was pressurized with air and you’re sealed in, like a moon suit kind of thing,

760
00:50:46.541 --> 00:50:52.532
just so that you don’t have any exposure if you had to go in what they call hot areas.

761
00:50:52.532 --> 00:50:54.108
So that was a job.

762
00:50:54.108 --> 00:50:56.000
We got through it. [laughs]

763
00:50:56.000 --> 00:50:57.975
<v DRAGOON>How did you get into that area?

764
00:50:57.975 --> 00:51:00.797
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Well, because I had worked at the Fells Research Institute.

765
00:51:00.797 --> 00:51:03.336
And like I said, that was not hot work.

766
00:51:03.336 --> 00:51:05.876
So it was just basically working with the samples.

767
00:51:05.876 --> 00:51:07.286
I started as a dishwasher.

768
00:51:07.286 --> 00:51:09.544
I was hired to clean the laboratory dishes.

769
00:51:09.544 --> 00:51:15.469
After being there for a while, fortunately I was working with someone who recognized I could do more than wash dishes.

770
00:51:15.469 --> 00:51:18.008
So then I actually started doing some sample prep.

771
00:51:18.008 --> 00:51:23.369
Most of the analysis we did, starting from that time and until I quit working, was all instrumental anyway.

772
00:51:23.369 --> 00:51:26.191
So then I started sample prepping and running the analysis.

773
00:51:26.191 --> 00:51:32.116
And she was really good about teaching me there, too, about the machines and how they work and what have you.

774
00:51:32.116 --> 00:51:36.913
So when I left Fells, I quit working with Fells because we had adopted our first child,

775
00:51:36.913 --> 00:51:39.734
my son, and I wanted to be home with him.

776
00:51:39.734 --> 00:51:43.685
I guess it was about two years, three years, I was home with him.

777
00:51:43.685 --> 00:51:46.506
And then decided I wanted to go back to work.

778
00:51:46.506 --> 00:51:51.303
When I started applying for work, then I got lucky enough to get a job with Monsanto.

779
00:51:51.303 --> 00:51:53.560
The disadvantage was that it was like hours,

780
00:51:53.560 --> 00:51:55.817
<c.p>about an hour away from where we lived.

781
00:51:55.817 --> 00:51:58.075
I had to drive all the way there.

782
00:51:58.075 --> 00:52:01.178
And that’s been the case with pretty much all my jobs.

783
00:52:01.178 --> 00:52:04.000
I was the street runner. [clock chiming] Sorry. [laughter]

784
00:52:04.000 --> 00:52:04.745
<v DRAGOON>That’s okay.

785
00:52:04.745 --> 00:52:06.607
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Fortunately it’s just two o’clock.

786
00:52:06.607 --> 00:52:07.352
<v DRAGOON>[laughs] Wow.

787
00:52:07.352 --> 00:52:08.841
<v LATOUR-PITRE>So like I said,

788
00:52:08.841 --> 00:52:18.897
when I . . . And about the same time I started to go back to work was about the same time I decided I was going back to school.

789
00:52:18.897 --> 00:52:21.131
So, ’76 . . . yeah, about ’76, ’77.

790
00:52:21.131 --> 00:52:25.972
Then I got picked up by Monsanto and worked there for eight years.

791
00:52:25.972 --> 00:52:27.834
Then we relocated to Virginia.

792
00:52:27.834 --> 00:52:33.421
And when I got to Virginia, because of the type of research I was doing,

793
00:52:33.421 --> 00:52:39.752
I kept hearing “It’s not compatible with the research we do at NIH,” and what have you.

794
00:52:39.752 --> 00:52:43.848
And it was like, “We don’t do weaponry research and development.”

795
00:52:43.848 --> 00:52:46.828
Ended up with a whole new career direction.

796
00:52:46.828 --> 00:52:52.414
Ended up doing consulting work for several consulting companies and environmental management for government contracts.

797
00:52:52.414 --> 00:52:57.255
And so then I spent another twenty years doing that kind of work.

798
00:52:57.255 --> 00:52:58.000
<v DRAGOON>Wow.

799
00:52:58.000 --> 00:52:59.017
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Keeps it interesting. [laughter]

800
00:52:59.017 --> 00:52:59.780
<v DRAGOON>Sounds like it.

801
00:52:59.780 --> 00:53:00.542
Sounds very interesting.

802
00:53:00.542 --> 00:53:06.898
<v LATOUR-PITRE>I don’t have to get bored doing it. [laughs] And in the meantime, when I got to Virginia, I thought I’ll have to do something.

803
00:53:06.898 --> 00:53:09.186
I thought, I know, I’ll get a realtor’s license.

804
00:53:09.186 --> 00:53:11.729
So I was a realtor for thirty seconds, too. [laughs]

805
00:53:11.729 --> 00:53:12.492
<v DRAGOON>Oh my goodness.

806
00:53:12.492 --> 00:53:13.000
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

807
00:53:13.000 --> 00:53:13.582
<v DRAGOON>Wow.

808
00:53:13.582 --> 00:53:14.745
That’s amazing.

809
00:53:14.745 --> 00:53:23.473
<v LATOUR-PITRE>My husband’s a very patient person. [laughter] Can I offer you ladies anything to drink?

810
00:53:23.473 --> 00:53:24.636
Or eat?

811
00:53:24.636 --> 00:53:25.800
You’re good?

812
00:53:25.800 --> 00:53:26.964
<v DRAGOON>It’s okay.

813
00:53:26.964 --> 00:53:28.709
Yeah, we’re good.

814
00:53:28.709 --> 00:53:29.873
Thank you.

815
00:53:29.873 --> 00:53:35.109
I have some water on me, so we’re good.

816
00:53:35.109 --> 00:53:36.855
Thank you, though.

817
00:53:36.855 --> 00:53:38.600
I appreciate it.

818
00:53:38.600 --> 00:53:39.764
All right.

819
00:53:39.764 --> 00:53:45.000
Let me look at this for a second.

820
00:53:45.000 --> 00:53:47.000
<v LATOUR-PITRE>So I was kind of like the prodigal child.

821
00:53:47.000 --> 00:53:49.444
I was the one who left home and didn’t come back.

822
00:53:49.444 --> 00:53:51.000
<v DRAGOON>Did everyone else stay in the area?

823
00:53:51.000 --> 00:53:51.667
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Pretty much, yeah.

824
00:53:51.667 --> 00:53:54.111
I was the only one who actually left and stayed gone.

825
00:53:54.111 --> 00:53:57.000
My oldest brother left for a while but came back when he married.

826
00:53:57.000 --> 00:53:58.778
He and his wife and family been here.

827
00:53:58.778 --> 00:53:59.667
My sister never left.

828
00:53:59.667 --> 00:54:00.111
Youngest brother.

829
00:54:00.111 --> 00:54:03.000
<v DRAGOON>Did you always know you wanted to come back to the area?

830
00:54:03.000 --> 00:54:03.616
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Oh, yeah.

831
00:54:03.616 --> 00:54:07.929
My thought was, when we left Ohio . . . Matter of fact, I tease my husband.

832
00:54:07.929 --> 00:54:14.091
I said, “If I knew you was going to move every twenty years, we could have stayed in the military.”

833
00:54:14.091 --> 00:54:18.404
I said, “My thought when we were going to move, we were going home.”

834
00:54:18.404 --> 00:54:20.561
But we went further away from home.

835
00:54:20.561 --> 00:54:25.490
When we got ready to retire, we started trying to decide where we wanted to live,

836
00:54:25.490 --> 00:54:31.652
because I knew I didn’t want to stay up in that . . . it’s really hustle bustle, cold weather and all that.

837
00:54:31.652 --> 00:54:34.732
I said, “I don’t really want to grow old here.”

838
00:54:34.732 --> 00:54:37.197
I want to move someplace where it’s warmer.

839
00:54:37.197 --> 00:54:39.045
And we looked at South Carolina.

840
00:54:39.045 --> 00:54:39.662
Loved it.

841
00:54:39.662 --> 00:54:41.510
But we were in South Carolina.

842
00:54:41.510 --> 00:54:43.975
I told him, I said, “You know what?”

843
00:54:43.975 --> 00:54:48.596
I said, “I miss my family and my extended family, and I miss my culture.

844
00:54:48.596 --> 00:54:50.136
I want to go home.”

845
00:54:50.136 --> 00:54:52.293
And so we decided to come home.

846
00:54:52.293 --> 00:54:55.990
And we moved . . . made the final move to come back in 2006.

847
00:54:55.990 --> 00:54:56.914
And it’s good.

848
00:54:56.914 --> 00:55:02.460
I miss my babies that are still in Reston, Virginia. [laughs] But we’ve been satisfied being back home.

849
00:55:02.460 --> 00:55:04.000
It keeps you busy.

850
00:55:04.000 --> 00:55:07.592
<v DRAGOON>Who . . . I’m going back to your childhood questions.

851
00:55:07.592 --> 00:55:08.041
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Okay.

852
00:55:08.041 --> 00:55:12.531
<v DRAGOON>Who were your role models when you were growing up?

853
00:55:12.531 --> 00:55:13.429
<v LATOUR-PITRE>My parents.

854
00:55:13.429 --> 00:55:14.327
My parents.

855
00:55:14.327 --> 00:55:24.653
And my mother’s father, as I got older and I looked back at him, there was such a cool, quiet wisdom about him.

856
00:55:24.653 --> 00:55:30.939
My dad’s father seemed to be angry a lot of . . . for the most part.

857
00:55:30.939 --> 00:55:35.429
But I think the anger was because of his handicap.

858
00:55:35.429 --> 00:55:40.367
I think he was a man who was like my dad.

859
00:55:40.367 --> 00:55:44.857
He was strong, go get it, and now I’m handicapped.

860
00:55:44.857 --> 00:55:49.347
So I think sometimes he would get frustrated with that.

861
00:55:49.347 --> 00:55:54.286
So you could see a lot of times he was frustrated.

862
00:55:54.286 --> 00:56:01.469
My mother’s father was a lot of, the laughter, it was such a big, hearty laugh.

863
00:56:01.469 --> 00:56:02.367
He laughed.

864
00:56:02.367 --> 00:56:04.163
He found things funny.

865
00:56:04.163 --> 00:56:11.796
He would play jokes on the other, on the older men, his brother and my dad’s dad.

866
00:56:11.796 --> 00:56:23.469
When he would come to visit us, the two old men would get together on the porch and play cards, and they would scream and yell.

867
00:56:23.469 --> 00:56:28.857
In fact, Joe would get mad and go home, and [?] would laugh.

868
00:56:28.857 --> 00:56:32.000
So he would mess with him.

869
00:56:32.000 --> 00:56:37.969
But when I think about the role model, yeah, my mom and my dad, really.

870
00:56:37.969 --> 00:56:45.133
My thing with my mom is, I tell people, I said, “I can understand the woman’s liberation movement.

871
00:56:45.133 --> 00:56:46.724
I understand the concept.”

872
00:56:46.724 --> 00:56:55.878
I said, “But for me, it was no great awakening, because the woman I grew up with had been liberated all my life.”

873
00:56:55.878 --> 00:57:02.245
And it was amazing because the thing that I liked about what I saw with her,

874
00:57:02.245 --> 00:57:07.418
and I tell young women that now, too, she was quietly in control.

875
00:57:07.418 --> 00:57:09.408
But she was in control.

876
00:57:09.408 --> 00:57:11.796
She, Dad, “Yeah, you the man.”

877
00:57:11.796 --> 00:57:20.949
And then it wasn’t important to her to try to show him that she was in charge or that she had the strength.

878
00:57:20.949 --> 00:57:26.918
When you got old and you realized what was going on, she changed his life.

879
00:57:26.918 --> 00:57:30.898
She helped us develop a reasonably stable life, sane life.

880
00:57:30.898 --> 00:57:39.255
So, yeah, she had a . . . and again, what it was, a quiet strength and wisdom that I saw with my grandfather.

881
00:57:39.255 --> 00:57:43.633
She didn’t have to rant and rail and joke and cuss.

882
00:57:43.633 --> 00:57:46.418
My father was a little cussing sailor.

883
00:57:46.418 --> 00:57:50.000
I said he cussed fluently in two languages.

884
00:57:50.000 --> 00:57:57.241
But the thing with my dad that we found, and my sister and I laugh about this a lot of times,

885
00:57:57.241 --> 00:58:03.103
is you’d come to him and was like, “Wah, wah, wah, my life” – even as we were

886
00:58:03.103 --> 00:58:04.828
<c.p>adult – “sucks, it’s so hard.”

887
00:58:04.828 --> 00:58:09.310
And Mom would try to talk you through it from a spiritual perspective.

888
00:58:09.310 --> 00:58:11.034
Not a lot of coddling.

889
00:58:11.034 --> 00:58:14.483
But she wanted to try to enlighten it for you.

890
00:58:14.483 --> 00:58:21.379
My dad will look at the situation, he says, “You can go with this, or you can go with that.”

891
00:58:21.379 --> 00:58:24.483
And in a way, it was like, hello, Eureka.

892
00:58:24.483 --> 00:58:30.000
Because that comes in handy so many times in our lives, that it’s not that complicated.

893
00:58:30.000 --> 00:58:30.690
Pick one.

894
00:58:30.690 --> 00:58:34.828
And once you do, I don’t want to hear about it anymore.

895
00:58:34.828 --> 00:58:38.621
And that was his attitude: “I don’t want to hear it.”

896
00:58:38.621 --> 00:58:41.379
Make a decision and then shut it down.

897
00:58:41.379 --> 00:58:45.862
And so, yeah, they both brought . . . set up examples for all of us,

898
00:58:45.862 --> 00:58:50.000
I’m sure, and I know for me, that I live by today.

899
00:58:50.000 --> 00:58:51.379
I’m thankful for it.

900
00:58:51.379 --> 00:58:53.103
Because it was well-balanced, too.

901
00:58:53.103 --> 00:58:57.241
So from a woman’s perspective, my mom would . . . that was the girl.

902
00:58:57.241 --> 00:58:59.310
She was a perfect role model.

903
00:58:59.310 --> 00:59:03.793
She knew how to be a liberated woman when she needed to be,

904
00:59:03.793 --> 00:59:08.621
and still the thing that she didn’t have to prove herself to anybody else.

905
00:59:08.621 --> 00:59:16.207
The sense of, this is where I’m at, this is what I have to do, this is what I’m going to do.

906
00:59:16.207 --> 00:59:20.000
And whether you know it or not, it didn’t matter.

907
00:59:20.000 --> 00:59:23.529
<v DRAGOON>It sounds like your parents were really supportive of each other, too?

908
00:59:23.529 --> 00:59:24.706
<v LATOUR-PITRE>I think they were.

909
00:59:24.706 --> 00:59:27.353
Like I said, there were differences, but . . . oh, yeah.

910
00:59:27.353 --> 00:59:29.706
And then patience and tolerance with each other.

911
00:59:29.706 --> 00:59:31.176
My dad was a partier.

912
00:59:31.176 --> 00:59:38.235
But my mom never got into these screaming matches with, “Where are you going again tonight,” and “You’re drinking” and “What are you doing?”

913
00:59:38.235 --> 00:59:39.118
None of that.

914
00:59:39.118 --> 00:59:41.176
It was like, “You’re a grown man.

915
00:59:41.176 --> 00:59:42.941
I’m going to say my prayers.

916
00:59:42.941 --> 00:59:44.118
I’m going to bed.”

917
00:59:44.118 --> 00:59:46.765
You know, “If you fall in the ditch, fine.

918
00:59:46.765 --> 00:59:52.647
When you wake up . . . ” It was none of that coddling and fighting about what he was doing and his lifestyle.

919
00:59:52.647 --> 00:59:53.529
None of it.

920
00:59:53.529 --> 00:59:58.824
And I think because of that, Dad came to terms with what he needed to do on his

921
00:59:58.824 --> 00:59:59.118
<c.p>own.

922
00:59:59.118 --> 01:00:06.471
So it was like once he realized okay, this is crazy, I can’t keep living like this, it wasn’t because somebody browbeat him into it.

923
01:00:06.471 --> 01:00:12.353
He got to a point where he realized this is not working, and it’s not the best thing to do.

924
01:00:12.353 --> 01:00:14.118
Even taking responsibility for his family.

925
01:00:14.118 --> 01:00:20.882
Because when he started to work at Firestone, someone had finally heard him play and had asked him if he wanted to travel,

926
01:00:20.882 --> 01:00:25.882
if he wanted to, and he assessed that idea and he talked to his dad about it.

927
01:00:25.882 --> 01:00:29.118
But he said, “I just got a job offer at Firestone.”

928
01:00:29.118 --> 01:00:33.824
So he made a decision to go to work for Firestone instead of being a musician.

929
01:00:33.824 --> 01:00:38.529
If he had decided to be a musician with the drinking and partying and carrying on,

930
01:00:38.529 --> 01:00:43.824
there’s no telling where that could have end up. [laughs] But she would quietly, “You’re a grown person.

931
01:00:43.824 --> 01:00:45.000
Make your own decisions.”

932
01:00:45.000 --> 01:00:46.471
And she would say that.

933
01:00:46.471 --> 01:00:49.118
“You’re the one who have to save your soul.

934
01:00:49.118 --> 01:00:50.000
Not me.”

935
01:00:50.000 --> 01:00:52.477
<v DRAGOON>Were there any big disagreements between them?

936
01:00:52.477 --> 01:00:55.662
Were there any things that they didn’t agree on?

937
01:00:55.662 --> 01:01:00.262
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Because of his education, see, she took care of the finances pretty much.

938
01:01:00.262 --> 01:01:03.446
But she respected this is money he had earned.

939
01:01:03.446 --> 01:01:11.585
So she made sure that she took what she needed to operate the household, and then left the rest of his money alone.

940
01:01:11.585 --> 01:01:18.308
He was open to that and willing to let that happen, because, again, of his educational level, I guess.

941
01:01:18.308 --> 01:01:28.569
So some of the issues that you might hear that people really would get into an argument about that could cause some concern of separation or anything like that,

942
01:01:28.569 --> 01:01:36.000
no. I never ever remember hearing them . . . There were strained periods, like when we were becoming teenagers and started to date.

943
01:01:36.000 --> 01:01:41.662
And my dad had to adjust to okay, guys are going to be . . . with a son,

944
01:01:41.662 --> 01:01:48.031
the oldest son and him starting to do this kind of thing, so she’s standing in the middle.

945
01:01:48.031 --> 01:01:49.800
Those kind of stress things.

946
01:01:49.800 --> 01:01:50.862
But no, they

947
01:01:50.862 --> 01:01:55.815
<c.p>never really got to a point where, they lived a quiet, private . . . Hi, honey!

948
01:01:55.815 --> 01:01:56.523
I’m sorry.

949
01:01:56.523 --> 01:01:57.585
Use your key!

950
01:01:57.585 --> 01:01:59.000
That’s my sister.

951
01:01:59.000 --> 01:02:00.167
<v CECELIA LATOUR>It’s locked.

952
01:02:00.167 --> 01:02:01.917
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Use your key.

953
01:02:01.917 --> 01:02:03.083
<v C. LATOUR>I can’t.

954
01:02:03.083 --> 01:02:05.417
My hands are full.

955
01:02:05.417 --> 01:02:06.583
<v LATOUR-PITRE>I’m wired.

956
01:02:06.583 --> 01:02:15.333
I can’t. [laughs] I’m going to introduce you to some . . . Oh, your hands are full.

957
01:02:15.333 --> 01:02:15.917
<v C. LATOUR>Hi.

958
01:02:15.917 --> 01:02:18.833
<v LATOUR-PITRE>This is my sister Cecelia.

959
01:02:18.833 --> 01:02:20.000
<v DRAGOON>Hello.

960
01:02:20.000 --> 01:02:25.824
<v LATOUR-PITRE>This is my grand-niece, Alexis, and my great grand-nephew.

961
01:02:25.824 --> 01:02:27.765
<v DRAGOON>Hi, I’m Stephanie.

962
01:02:27.765 --> 01:02:29.706
Hi little one!

963
01:02:29.706 --> 01:02:31.647
Oh, so cute!

964
01:02:31.647 --> 01:02:34.235
<v LATOUR-PITRE>He is a cutie.

965
01:02:34.235 --> 01:02:35.529
<v DRAGOON>Okay, yes.

966
01:02:35.529 --> 01:02:36.824
<v C. LATOUR>Cecelia LaTour.

967
01:02:36.824 --> 01:02:38.118
<v DRAGOON>All right.

968
01:02:38.118 --> 01:02:38.765
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Anne.

969
01:02:38.765 --> 01:02:39.412
<v C. LATOUR>Hmm?

970
01:02:39.412 --> 01:02:40.706
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Cecelia Anne.

971
01:02:40.706 --> 01:02:42.647
<v C. LATOUR>Oh, Cecelia Anne.

972
01:02:42.647 --> 01:02:43.941
My bad.

973
01:02:43.941 --> 01:02:45.882
Cecelia Anne LaTour.

974
01:02:45.882 --> 01:02:47.176
<v DRAGOON>All right.

975
01:02:47.176 --> 01:02:49.118
<v C. LATOUR>Class of ‘65.

976
01:02:49.118 --> 01:02:53.000
That’s what you all need.

977
01:02:53.000 --> 01:02:54.034
<v DRAGOON>Thank you.

978
01:02:54.034 --> 01:02:58.690
So we were talking about growing up in Mossville.

979
01:02:58.690 --> 01:03:01.276
Where did we leave off?

980
01:03:01.276 --> 01:03:08.000
<v LATOUR-PITRE>We were talking about the relationship between my parents, and role models.

981
01:03:08.000 --> 01:03:08.321
<v DRAGOON>Yeah.

982
01:03:08.321 --> 01:03:12.491
I also wanted to ask some questions about going to school in Mossville.

983
01:03:12.491 --> 01:03:14.094
What was your experience like?

984
01:03:14.094 --> 01:03:22.113
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah, and when you asked me about role models, I’m going to jump ahead and then kind of give you my quick version of it.

985
01:03:22.113 --> 01:03:22.434
<v DRAGOON>Sure.

986
01:03:22.434 --> 01:03:28.849
<v LATOUR-PITRE>One of the teachers, and it turned out ironically I ended up with a degree in chemistry, was Ms. Spikes.

987
01:03:28.849 --> 01:03:34.623
And it was like, every now and then you have one that really makes this impression on you.

988
01:03:34.623 --> 01:03:36.547
There was Mr. Mollo, who died.

989
01:03:36.547 --> 01:03:38.792
These are like two of the people.

990
01:03:38.792 --> 01:03:41.358
And Ms. Rogers . . . was she the English teacher?

991
01:03:41.358 --> 01:03:42.000
<v C. LATOUR>[Agrees]

992
01:03:42.000 --> 01:03:48.807
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Macbeth had got burned into my brain and it was like . . . and I’d go home and it was like,

993
01:03:48.807 --> 01:03:50.599
my family is like Macbeth.

994
01:03:50.599 --> 01:03:52.032
Are you for real?

995
01:03:52.032 --> 01:03:54.898
I’d be sitting there watching Macbeth on TV.

996
01:03:54.898 --> 01:03:59.556
Because for some reason, she made that impression and it stayed with me.

997
01:03:59.556 --> 01:04:07.079
But Ms. Spikes was really a cool teacher in terms of, when I thought back about why am I taking chemistry?

998
01:04:07.079 --> 01:04:12.454
And one time she had made a comment about, “You ought to go into science.”

999
01:04:12.454 --> 01:04:15.320
<c.p>I’m going like, “No. I don’t do science.”

1000
01:04:15.320 --> 01:04:18.186
It was like, never was interested in science.

1001
01:04:18.186 --> 01:04:19.977
So it’s kind of interesting.

1002
01:04:19.977 --> 01:04:25.351
I guess somehow or another she . . . But I remember I was really impressed with her.

1003
01:04:25.351 --> 01:04:26.785
She made an impression.

1004
01:04:26.785 --> 01:04:28.576
Mr. Mollo was another one.

1005
01:04:28.576 --> 01:04:32.875
But when we got to Mossville, Mossville School was just finished building.

1006
01:04:32.875 --> 01:04:35.741
And of course around here it’s always hot.

1007
01:04:35.741 --> 01:04:41.116
I can remember the hot summer days, and going to school and going to class.

1008
01:04:41.116 --> 01:04:43.982
But as it progressed through the school year,

1009
01:04:43.982 --> 01:04:51.147
I was probably one of the ones with minimal involvement and not a lot of extended friendships in the school.

1010
01:04:51.147 --> 01:04:59.029
Again, that’s one of those things that we talk about every now and then is like, I had kind of isolated myself.

1011
01:04:59.029 --> 01:05:01.896
It was like, I had very few friends.

1012
01:05:01.896 --> 01:05:07.986
Lenoria, the Braxton who lived right down the street, she turned out to be one of them.

1013
01:05:07.986 --> 01:05:11.927
My brother’s wife, she and I developed a friendship by then.

1014
01:05:11.927 --> 01:05:13.719
We were in high school.

1015
01:05:13.719 --> 01:05:19.810
But pretty much just went to school and dreaded every Monday, having to go back to school.

1016
01:05:19.810 --> 01:05:21.959
I was one of those kids.

1017
01:05:21.959 --> 01:05:24.109
But failure was not an option.

1018
01:05:24.109 --> 01:05:25.900
You don’t fail a grade.

1019
01:05:25.900 --> 01:05:30.916
So you’re there for a reason, so get her done, like I said before.

1020
01:05:30.916 --> 01:05:32.707
So I got through it.

1021
01:05:32.707 --> 01:05:35.932
Didn’t participate in a lot of the extracurricular activities.

1022
01:05:35.932 --> 01:05:39.873
I don't think maybe any of them that I can remember.

1023
01:05:39.873 --> 01:05:42.023
So I was the studious type.

1024
01:05:42.023 --> 01:05:48.472
I just stayed with my books. [laughs] But by the time you graduate, there’s a school pride there.

1025
01:05:48.472 --> 01:05:51.338
And certainly after you leave, you start appreciating.

1026
01:05:51.338 --> 01:05:53.846
I’ll just say this, for the record,

1027
01:05:53.846 --> 01:06:04.952
I think for me what I found out after I left Mossville and the schools started to integrate and you ended up with the integration of schools on a large scale,

1028
01:06:04.952 --> 01:06:11.401
when I think back about it, I think the thing that Mossville offered us as a minority community,

1029
01:06:11.401 --> 01:06:16.417
and minority children . . . [phone rings] Okay, let me see if I can . . . I’m sorry.

1030
01:06:16.417 --> 01:06:16.776
Okay.

1031
01:06:16.776 --> 01:06:20.000
I think that will take care of it.

1032
01:06:20.000 --> 01:06:26.009
What I realized was being in an all-black school was a real benefit, I think, for me,

1033
01:06:26.009 --> 01:06:33.079
I will say that, because I think the support we had in the school, a lot from the teachers as

1034
01:06:33.079 --> 01:06:37.320
<c.p>well as you’re in a community where you all know each other.

1035
01:06:37.320 --> 01:06:41.562
We’re from the community, and it was a small community, small school.

1036
01:06:41.562 --> 01:06:43.329
That also, I think, helped.

1037
01:06:43.329 --> 01:06:45.804
But the teachers were really good teachers.

1038
01:06:45.804 --> 01:06:53.580
They were really intent on trying to make us understand and teach us how to advance and go out into the world.

1039
01:06:53.580 --> 01:06:57.115
And we had a lot of that one on one.

1040
01:06:57.115 --> 01:06:59.236
There was a sense of pride.

1041
01:06:59.236 --> 01:07:04.891
Our teachers were very proud of who they were, their accomplishments, and that they were educators.

1042
01:07:04.891 --> 01:07:05.952
They dressed appropriately.

1043
01:07:05.952 --> 01:07:07.012
They demanded respect.

1044
01:07:07.012 --> 01:07:10.193
We were ordered and made sure they got respect.

1045
01:07:10.193 --> 01:07:13.021
Everybody from the bus driver to the janitors.

1046
01:07:13.021 --> 01:07:21.151
So it set up a respect and understanding of ourselves, and the fact that we happened to be African Americans or the minority,

1047
01:07:21.151 --> 01:07:25.039
that gave us a sense of pride in who we were.

1048
01:07:25.039 --> 01:07:30.341
When you’re accomplishing in an environment like that, then you get that sense of accomplishing.

1049
01:07:30.341 --> 01:07:39.532
You don’t have a teacher looking at you thirty seconds after you’re in a classroom and going like, “You look like you might have trouble communicating.

1050
01:07:39.532 --> 01:07:43.773
I think we’re going to . . . ” We didn’t have that kind of stuff.

1051
01:07:43.773 --> 01:07:45.894
You either learned how to communicate.

1052
01:07:45.894 --> 01:07:49.429
And they help you to figure out how to communicate.

1053
01:07:49.429 --> 01:07:51.903
So there was an advantage, I felt.

1054
01:07:51.903 --> 01:08:00.740
After I started seeing what happened to minority children once the schools were completely integrated, they get lost in such a sea of everybody else.

1055
01:08:00.740 --> 01:08:14.526
And I think maybe in a lot of cases a genuine lack of understanding of what’s needed for these kids so that you can motivate them the right way and get them and turn them in the right direction,

1056
01:08:14.526 --> 01:08:15.586
that’s not there.

1057
01:08:15.586 --> 01:08:17.000
It gets missed.

1058
01:08:17.000 --> 01:08:23.528
When that happens, now you have a kid looking at why am I here, I don't know why I’m here.

1059
01:08:23.528 --> 01:08:28.751
If I was in a fog when I was in high school surrounded by my community,

1060
01:08:28.751 --> 01:08:34.953
you can imagine what it might be like for a kid who’s maybe like me in an integrated community,

1061
01:08:34.953 --> 01:08:38.218
where nobody is stopping to say hey, you’re okay here.

1062
01:08:38.218 --> 01:08:38.870
You’re safe.

1063
01:08:38.870 --> 01:08:40.503
You can feel comfortable here.

1064
01:08:40.503 --> 01:08:43.440
And I think sometimes that’s what the problem is.

1065
01:08:43.440 --> 01:08:45.073
They don’t feel safe, they

1066
01:08:45.073 --> 01:08:50.622
<c.p>don’t feel comfortable, because it’s just too big, it’s too overwhelming, and it’s just not nurturing enough.

1067
01:08:50.622 --> 01:08:53.233
Maybe that just happened across the board overall.

1068
01:08:53.233 --> 01:08:57.803
But from a minority perspective and what I got out of being at Mossville,

1069
01:08:57.803 --> 01:09:03.352
that’s what I see for the minority children who are now not in an environment like that.

1070
01:09:03.352 --> 01:09:11.513
So Mossville provided us not only an environment for education and for learning, but for confidence building and for security, being in an all-black school.

1071
01:09:11.513 --> 01:09:20.000
I think that’s what I got out of being in school there the most, even if I didn’t participate in a lot of extracurricular. [laughs]

1072
01:09:20.000 --> 01:09:24.706
<v DRAGOON>So was school integration happening after your time in Mossville?

1073
01:09:24.706 --> 01:09:26.588
<v LATOUR-PITRE>After I was . . . yeah.

1074
01:09:26.588 --> 01:09:28.471
I graduated in ’66.

1075
01:09:28.471 --> 01:09:30.824
The integration started . . . late ‘60s?

1076
01:09:30.824 --> 01:09:34.588
<v C. LATOUR>When they integrated the school it was in . . .

1077
01:09:34.588 --> 01:09:36.000
<v LATOUR-PITRE>‘69, ‘70?

1078
01:09:36.000 --> 01:09:43.480
<v C. LATOUR>Well, not even integrate Mossville, but when the kids started going to other schools it was ‘68.

1079
01:09:43.480 --> 01:09:43.920
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

1080
01:09:43.920 --> 01:09:46.120
It wasn’t too long after . . .

1081
01:09:46.120 --> 01:09:49.640
<v C. LATOUR>The last graduating class from Mossville was ‘68.

1082
01:09:49.640 --> 01:09:50.080
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Okay.

1083
01:09:50.080 --> 01:09:54.480
So, yeah, after ‘68, then the high school peeled off.

1084
01:09:54.480 --> 01:09:56.240
The high school integrated.

1085
01:09:56.240 --> 01:10:00.200
<v C. LATOUR>High school kids started going either Sulphur or Westlake.

1086
01:10:00.200 --> 01:10:00.640
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Right.

1087
01:10:00.640 --> 01:10:08.120
<v C. LATOUR>It remained a junior high school and then it became just an elementary until they closed it.

1088
01:10:08.120 --> 01:10:09.000
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Right.

1089
01:10:09.000 --> 01:10:13.151
<v DRAGOON>So the closure of the high school was directly related to school integration?

1090
01:10:13.151 --> 01:10:13.790
<v LATOUR-PITRE>[Agrees] Yeah.

1091
01:10:13.790 --> 01:10:18.261
And because of the separation and the quality of education, integration needed to happen.

1092
01:10:18.261 --> 01:10:21.134
I’m not saying that I regret that integration occurred.

1093
01:10:21.134 --> 01:10:25.605
I think it was a good idea that probably wasn’t executed, or has not,

1094
01:10:25.605 --> 01:10:29.756
certainly at this point, not being executed as well as it could be.

1095
01:10:29.756 --> 01:10:30.395
I don’t . . .

1096
01:10:30.395 --> 01:10:37.420
<v C. LATOUR>It was more of an equality because of what we would get at our school . . . like when we were in high school,

1097
01:10:37.420 --> 01:10:44.445
the books that we would receive would be books that either Westlake or Sulphur had already used for quite a few years.

1098
01:10:44.445 --> 01:10:47.958
We never got new books; we always would get their books.

1099
01:10:47.958 --> 01:10:52.109
So what they were getting, by the time we got that same thing,

1100
01:10:52.109 --> 01:10:57.538
we were like five or ten years behind as far as what’s being taught in that book.

1101
01:10:57.538 --> 01:11:02.328
So I think it was more of if you’re sitting in a classroom with somebody,

1102
01:11:02.328 --> 01:11:06.798
be they white or black, we need to be all getting the same thing.

1103
01:11:06.798 --> 01:11:09.034
And that was how they settled that.

1104
01:11:09.034 --> 01:11:13.824
Okay, well if you want to get the same education, equal at the same time,

1105
01:11:13.824 --> 01:11:17.975
then we’re going to have to start putting you sitting out over here.

1106
01:11:17.975 --> 01:11:25.000
But the black kids were the ones that had to make that adjustment and move, because no white kids came to Mossville.

1107
01:11:25.000 --> 01:11:29.151
So they didn’t have to . . . If they lived across the street from Mossville,

1108
01:11:29.151 --> 01:11:32.983
they didn’t have to go to Mossville, they could go somewhere else.

1109
01:11:32.983 --> 01:11:37.773
If we lived across the street from Westlake or Sulphur, we were bused to Mossville.

1110
01:11:37.773 --> 01:11:38.092
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Right.

1111
01:11:38.092 --> 01:11:45.118
<v C. LATOUR>So the education that, what we were learning, and it didn’t hurt us at all, like she said, the instruction we had . . .

1112
01:11:45.118 --> 01:11:50.227
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Well I think it goes back to the teachers and the quality of teachers we had.

1113
01:11:50.227 --> 01:11:50.546
<v C. LATOUR>Right.

1114
01:11:50.546 --> 01:11:54.697
With the teachers and what we had to work with, we did fine.

1115
01:11:54.697 --> 01:11:58.529
But that was more of, the fuss came about with the integration.

1116
01:11:58.529 --> 01:12:00.445
Because we wanted to have equality.

1117
01:12:00.445 --> 01:12:03.000
<v LATOUR-PITRE>It was inequality in educational levels, yeah.

1118
01:12:03.000 --> 01:12:07.673
<v C. LATOUR>If you want the same, and once my kids started school, they went to school in Mossville.

1119
01:12:07.673 --> 01:12:11.795
And that was my worst . . . When I was in school, I didn’t have no choice.

1120
01:12:11.795 --> 01:12:14.269
I had to learn what they would give me.

1121
01:12:14.269 --> 01:12:17.018
You’re sitting in the classroom with these kids every day.

1122
01:12:17.018 --> 01:12:20.316
What they teaching those kids is what you need to be learning.

1123
01:12:20.316 --> 01:12:24.713
So you can’t use that as an excuse anymore, because everybody learning the same thing now.

1124
01:12:24.713 --> 01:12:31.035
And I think that’s kind of how segregation really was like, okay, wait a minute, our kids are not getting the same thing.

1125
01:12:31.035 --> 01:12:34.608
But like she said earlier, our kids still missed out on a lot.

1126
01:12:34.608 --> 01:12:39.281
They had to adjust to go to this new school to get . . . to integrate with these people.

1127
01:12:39.281 --> 01:12:43.404
First time I ever went to an integrated school was when I was at McNeese.

1128
01:12:43.404 --> 01:12:46.702
Because all my life, I’d gone to school with all black kids.

1129
01:12:46.702 --> 01:12:50.000
So that was one of those things where there was just . . .

1130
01:12:50.000 --> 01:12:53.742
<v LATOUR-PITRE>And the reality about the integration is, and during that era, I mean,

1131
01:12:53.742 --> 01:13:00.364
it’s well known that there was a basic concern and fear that black people had . . . the minorities had of being in that environment.

1132
01:13:00.364 --> 01:13:02.379
And like I said, am I safe?

1133
01:13:02.379 --> 01:13:04.970
Is something bad going to happen to me here?

1134
01:13:04.970 --> 01:13:07.273
So when you walk into a learning institution,

1135
01:13:07.273 --> 01:13:13.030
you already . . . It’s like when the kids were standing at the step and the governor’s standing in the front saying,

1136
01:13:13.030 --> 01:13:14.470
“You’re not coming in here.”

1137
01:13:14.470 --> 01:13:18.788
You walk into a learning institution already afraid of what’s going to happen to you.

1138
01:13:18.788 --> 01:13:23.106
Well that’s kind of like a big veil that’s in front of why you’re there.

1139
01:13:23.106 --> 01:13:30.015
And so that was some of the kind of things that they had to deal with and adjust to that we didn’t have to.

1140
01:13:30.015 --> 01:13:32.030
And I think we got lucky, too.

1141
01:13:32.030 --> 01:13:37.500
Looked like when they built Mossville, when I go back and I think about the school, we got lucky.

1142
01:13:37.500 --> 01:13:39.227
The lab was nice and big.

1143
01:13:39.227 --> 01:13:40.667
You remember that science lab?

1144
01:13:40.667 --> 01:13:40.955
<v C. LATOUR>Yeah. [?]

1145
01:13:40.955 --> 01:13:43.833
<v LATOUR-PITRE>We ended up with, yeah, when they built the school . . .

1146
01:13:43.833 --> 01:13:45.561
<v C. LATOUR>It was a very nice school.

1147
01:13:45.561 --> 01:13:46.424
We had everything.

1148
01:13:46.424 --> 01:13:47.000
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

1149
01:13:47.000 --> 01:13:49.395
<v C. LATOUR>We were a little behind in certain things, but that’s what you get.

1150
01:13:49.395 --> 01:13:51.605
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Well like you said, the materials that we used was not equal.

1151
01:13:51.605 --> 01:13:51.974
<v C. LATOUR>The materials.

1152
01:13:51.974 --> 01:13:54.000
But the school itself, the lab, the home ec office.

1153
01:13:54.000 --> 01:13:58.486
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah, we had a kitchen for home ec and everything.

1154
01:13:58.486 --> 01:14:04.766
And I think in terms of funding, again, it goes back to our teachers.

1155
01:14:04.766 --> 01:14:11.944
They made do with what they had available to them and made the most of it,

1156
01:14:11.944 --> 01:14:20.019
where we probably weren’t getting the same kind of funding as some of the other kinds of schools.

1157
01:14:20.019 --> 01:14:22.262
But like I said again,

1158
01:14:22.262 --> 01:14:32.579
everybody from the bus drivers down to the teachers and the janitors tried to make sure that we recognized this was a good,

1159
01:14:32.579 --> 01:14:36.168
safe place to be, and it’s for learning.

1160
01:14:36.168 --> 01:14:37.065
<v C. LATOUR>[ . . . ?] toy boxes.

1161
01:14:37.065 --> 01:14:42.000
<v LATOUR-PITRE>You can move around with him if you want to.

1162
01:14:42.000 --> 01:14:44.571
<v DRAGOON>And you graduated, you said, in 1966?

1163
01:14:44.571 --> 01:14:46.408
<v LATOUR-PITRE>I got married in ‘66.

1164
01:14:46.408 --> 01:14:47.878
I graduated in ‘64.

1165
01:14:47.878 --> 01:14:48.980
<v DRAGOON>Oh, I’m sorry.

1166
01:14:48.980 --> 01:14:49.714
Oh, ‘64.

1167
01:14:49.714 --> 01:14:50.082
Okay.

1168
01:14:50.082 --> 01:14:53.755
So that was the year before the Voting Rights Act.

1169
01:14:53.755 --> 01:14:54.122
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

1170
01:14:54.122 --> 01:15:00.000
<v DRAGOON>Can you tell me a little bit about what that time was like for you?

1171
01:15:00.000 --> 01:15:06.609
<v LATOUR-PITRE>It was . . . like she said, when we got to McNeese, too, it was that awakening for all of us.

1172
01:15:06.609 --> 01:15:12.522
Everybody, most of us started to feel and recognize that no, we wanted to make the change.

1173
01:15:12.522 --> 01:15:20.522
And then when you saw the atrocities that was going on, then of course that always fuel some kind of feeling about that.

1174
01:15:20.522 --> 01:15:24.348
But I think I would have to say . . . [coughing] You find . . . ?

1175
01:15:24.348 --> 01:15:26.435
She going to find some water.

1176
01:15:26.435 --> 01:15:29.913
<v C. LATOUR>I don't know, she choking on that jalapeno pepper again.

1177
01:15:29.913 --> 01:15:30.957
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Oh, maybe so.

1178
01:15:30.957 --> 01:15:33.391
The water jugs are on the counter.

1179
01:15:33.391 --> 01:15:36.870
But again, being in the school environment was one thing.

1180
01:15:36.870 --> 01:15:41.043
But then as we would go out to the community, one of

1181
01:15:41.043 --> 01:15:46.957
<c.p>our big contribution, my sister and I, was we decided, we were going to Saint John Bosco.

1182
01:15:46.957 --> 01:15:49.739
And they had just built the new church.

1183
01:15:49.739 --> 01:15:52.870
And it was in the shape of a cross.

1184
01:15:52.870 --> 01:15:58.435
We were in the wings on the side, and then the main church, it was segregated.

1185
01:15:58.435 --> 01:16:05.391
We decided one Sunday we would, because everybody was, you started to see that small trickle of people, “I’m here.

1186
01:16:05.391 --> 01:16:06.087
I’m here.”

1187
01:16:06.087 --> 01:16:11.652
And fortunately our community, our neighborhood, didn’t get as bad as it did in some places.

1188
01:16:11.652 --> 01:16:15.130
Some of Westlake, I understand, got a little bit hairy.

1189
01:16:15.130 --> 01:16:20.000
But I mean, it wasn’t as bad as, you heard so many other.

1190
01:16:20.000 --> 01:16:24.898
And when she and I decided we wanted to sit in front, again, with my parents,

1191
01:16:24.898 --> 01:16:30.408
my mom’s attitude was hey, new adventure, go for it! [laughs] She was the one who was reading.

1192
01:16:30.408 --> 01:16:31.020
She understood.

1193
01:16:31.020 --> 01:16:34.388
And my dad understood, too, but my dad was more protective.

1194
01:16:34.388 --> 01:16:37.143
And his thing was, I work with these people.

1195
01:16:37.143 --> 01:16:42.347
If you go there and you start some mess, I’m going to have to work with them.

1196
01:16:42.347 --> 01:16:49.694
And of course I know in the back of his heart he’s thinking like, I’m going to have to defend you if something happens.

1197
01:16:49.694 --> 01:16:51.531
Like why are you doing this?

1198
01:16:51.531 --> 01:16:52.449
You’re stirring up.

1199
01:16:52.449 --> 01:16:55.510
But through conversations with her, again, sees black and white.

1200
01:16:55.510 --> 01:16:55.816
Fine.

1201
01:16:55.816 --> 01:16:56.735
Go for it.

1202
01:16:56.735 --> 01:17:04.388
So I know for me, my big challenge in overcoming fear was to walk in there and sit in that front part of the church,

1203
01:17:04.388 --> 01:17:06.224
towards the front of the church.

1204
01:17:06.224 --> 01:17:09.286
And she and I decided to do that one Sunday.

1205
01:17:09.286 --> 01:17:12.653
Well, we got the looks and the stares, and maybe somebody . . .

1206
01:17:12.653 --> 01:17:13.571
<v C. LATOUR>And the words.

1207
01:17:13.571 --> 01:17:14.490
<v LATOUR-PITRE>But, you know . . .

1208
01:17:14.490 --> 01:17:16.327
<v C. LATOUR>And we were called the words.

1209
01:17:16.327 --> 01:17:17.551
The magic word. [laughs]

1210
01:17:17.551 --> 01:17:20.000
<v LATOUR-PITRE>But nobody really physically came after us.

1211
01:17:20.000 --> 01:17:21.333
<v DRAGOON>How old were you?

1212
01:17:21.333 --> 01:17:22.333
<v LATOUR-PITRE>I guess seventeen?

1213
01:17:22.333 --> 01:17:22.667
Eighteen?

1214
01:17:22.667 --> 01:17:24.333
<v C. LATOUR>We was in high school.

1215
01:17:24.333 --> 01:17:26.000
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah, seventeen, eighteen, I guess.

1216
01:17:26.000 --> 01:17:26.667
And then . . .

1217
01:17:26.667 --> 01:17:27.667
<v C. LATOUR>It wasn’t eighteen.

1218
01:17:27.667 --> 01:17:29.000
I graduated at seventeen.

1219
01:17:29.000 --> 01:17:31.667
So it must have been fifteen and sixteen.

1220
01:17:31.667 --> 01:17:32.333
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah, okay.

1221
01:17:32.333 --> 01:17:38.000
Maybe at least sixteen, I guess, I was, probably at least, when we decided to do that.

1222
01:17:38.000 --> 01:17:43.000
But I think for me, and then in the small community, because again, by sixteen,

1223
01:17:43.000 --> 01:17:48.667
I was in the area and then by ‘66, 1966, I was married and I was gone.

1224
01:17:48.667 --> 01:17:55.000
And moving up to Ohio, one of the great questions was, “What is it like living in the South?”

1225
01:17:55.000 --> 01:18:03.333
And it was interesting because most of the idea people had, based on what they were seeing, is that we all lived in ghetto areas.

1226
01:18:03.333 --> 01:18:07.333
A lot of the people that I talked to, they assumed that.

1227
01:18:07.333 --> 01:18:11.333
I said, “I never live in a ghetto in my whole life.

1228
01:18:11.333 --> 01:18:18.000
My grandfather got thirty-something acres of land out in the field, and I grew up visiting them on the farm.

1229
01:18:18.000 --> 01:18:19.333
And cousins and aunts.

1230
01:18:19.333 --> 01:18:21.667
And we lived on all this land.”

1231
01:18:21.667 --> 01:18:27.333
It was like, “I don't know what it’s like living in a ghetto, in an apartment complex.

1232
01:18:27.333 --> 01:18:28.000
I can’t

1233
01:18:28.000 --> 01:18:29.000
<c.p>tell you that.”

1234
01:18:29.000 --> 01:18:36.000
So that’s when I realized like oh, okay, maybe my life was a little bit different than what people [assume?].

1235
01:18:36.000 --> 01:18:41.756
But what I would tell them was my experience with living in the South was racism is either covert or overt.

1236
01:18:41.756 --> 01:18:43.401
And I can deal with overt.

1237
01:18:43.401 --> 01:18:47.787
I know you hate me, I see you hate me, I know where you’re coming from.

1238
01:18:47.787 --> 01:18:52.447
If you call me a dirty word, call me the “N” word and walk away from me,

1239
01:18:52.447 --> 01:18:56.010
as long as you don’t touch me, I get that, I understand that.

1240
01:18:56.010 --> 01:19:04.782
But when you don’t even recognize yourself that you got a block or a prejudice and you’re telling me and you’re telling yourself I’m cool until you really need support from me,

1241
01:19:04.782 --> 01:19:08.345
I need support from you, I need you to make the right decision.

1242
01:19:08.345 --> 01:19:12.731
It’s like okay, you guys are a lot harder to deal with than in the South.

1243
01:19:12.731 --> 01:19:15.198
I know where they’re coming from in the South.

1244
01:19:15.198 --> 01:19:19.036
I know if you hate me what I need to do to protect myself.

1245
01:19:19.036 --> 01:19:26.162
With you guys, I think I’ve got a friend and then all of a sudden you say something to me or you pull something on me.

1246
01:19:26.162 --> 01:19:30.000
This is what I told them, that’s how I explained it to them.

1247
01:19:30.000 --> 01:19:34.522
But what I got out of that was a decision and a determination.

1248
01:19:34.522 --> 01:19:40.087
And I think that comes from our family background as well, that, not to be intimidated.

1249
01:19:40.087 --> 01:19:46.000
I didn’t put myself in a position where I made it easy for somebody to harm me.

1250
01:19:46.000 --> 01:19:51.565
I didn’t go out looking or making . . . It’s like I tell my kids all the time,

1251
01:19:51.565 --> 01:19:57.130
“You don’t have to keep screaming that you’re a minority or that you’re an African American.

1252
01:19:57.130 --> 01:20:00.261
All they’ve got to do is look at you.”

1253
01:20:00.261 --> 01:20:02.000
I am what I am.

1254
01:20:02.000 --> 01:20:05.826
It’s like, until they disrespect you, until they handle you, stand.

1255
01:20:05.826 --> 01:20:07.913
I started thinking about my ancestry.

1256
01:20:07.913 --> 01:20:12.435
Some of the people who hate me . . . family came here through Ellis Island.

1257
01:20:12.435 --> 01:20:17.652
We have family history back pre-Civil War. [laughs] It’s like no, this is my country.

1258
01:20:17.652 --> 01:20:18.348
No, wait!

1259
01:20:18.348 --> 01:20:24.261
And when I started adopting that attitude, and it’s like a lot of the people in Mossville.

1260
01:20:24.261 --> 01:20:27.043
That community got settled hundreds of years ago.

1261
01:20:27.043 --> 01:20:32.957
And I think the sense of pride that you see that, I think about when I think

1262
01:20:32.957 --> 01:20:36.435
<c.p>about Mossville, good, bad or indifferent, there is a pride.

1263
01:20:36.435 --> 01:20:44.435
Because that little community never would have survived as long as it did if it wasn’t a strong sense of pride in community,

1264
01:20:44.435 --> 01:20:46.522
that we’re here, we’ve been here.

1265
01:20:46.522 --> 01:20:52.087
This is . . . If you want to start claiming this is your country, this is our country.

1266
01:20:52.087 --> 01:20:55.217
I don’t have to apologize to you for that.

1267
01:20:55.217 --> 01:20:58.000
I helped . . . My ancestors helped build this.

1268
01:20:58.000 --> 01:21:02.673
So once I adopted that attitude and I tried to pass that on to my children.

1269
01:21:02.673 --> 01:21:04.133
Be ever aware of racism.

1270
01:21:04.133 --> 01:21:09.681
But you don’t have to wear it on your forehead or scream all the time about who you are.

1271
01:21:09.681 --> 01:21:11.434
They can see who you are.

1272
01:21:11.434 --> 01:21:12.894
They know who you are.

1273
01:21:12.894 --> 01:21:15.522
All you need them to do is respect you.

1274
01:21:15.522 --> 01:21:18.150
The rest of it, you got to go forward.

1275
01:21:18.150 --> 01:21:21.071
And again, I really think that comes from my parents.

1276
01:21:21.071 --> 01:21:23.699
Because they had to struggle, too, with the language.

1277
01:21:23.699 --> 01:21:24.575
My dad, especially.

1278
01:21:24.575 --> 01:21:29.832
He had to learn how to . . . When he first moved in this area, he could barely speak English.

1279
01:21:29.832 --> 01:21:31.000
<v C. LATOUR>Couldn’t at all.

1280
01:21:31.000 --> 01:21:31.311
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

1281
01:21:31.311 --> 01:21:33.487
And they would tease him a lot.

1282
01:21:33.487 --> 01:21:34.731
<v DRAGOON>What did he speak?

1283
01:21:34.731 --> 01:21:35.042
<v C. LATOUR>Creole.

1284
01:21:35.042 --> 01:21:35.975
<v LATOUR-PITRE>He spoke French.

1285
01:21:35.975 --> 01:21:36.286
<v DRAGOON>Wow.

1286
01:21:36.286 --> 01:21:36.597
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

1287
01:21:36.597 --> 01:21:37.840
Yeah, he spoke French.

1288
01:21:37.840 --> 01:21:39.395
And that’s all he spoke.

1289
01:21:39.395 --> 01:21:44.992
And with a fourth grade education and then being pulled out of school, all he spoke was French.

1290
01:21:44.992 --> 01:21:49.345
So he went through a period of learning to speak English and being teased.

1291
01:21:49.345 --> 01:21:50.277
<v C. LATOUR>[ . . . ?] many different ways.

1292
01:21:50.277 --> 01:21:52.765
When we were in school, we were fair.

1293
01:21:52.765 --> 01:21:54.630
My hair was kind of curly.

1294
01:21:54.630 --> 01:22:02.403
And when we were in school with the black kids, we were teased, because you’re too black to be white, too white to be black.

1295
01:22:02.403 --> 01:22:04.269
So we were teased a lot.

1296
01:22:04.269 --> 01:22:11.420
So it’s kind of like, again, like she go back, like our parents, know who you are, be proud of who you are.

1297
01:22:11.420 --> 01:22:12.664
And keep it moving.

1298
01:22:12.664 --> 01:22:15.462
It’s not important what somebody else thinks of you.

1299
01:22:15.462 --> 01:22:19.815
If somebody else would say something about us, it was like, that’s their problem.

1300
01:22:19.815 --> 01:22:24.168
What you think about me and how you feel about me is your problem.

1301
01:22:24.168 --> 01:22:26.345
I have nothing to do with that.

1302
01:22:26.345 --> 01:22:27.588
And that’s your right.

1303
01:22:27.588 --> 01:22:28.832
But you respect me.

1304
01:22:28.832 --> 01:22:30.387
And that, again, comes from . . .

1305
01:22:30.387 --> 01:22:30.697
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

1306
01:22:30.697 --> 01:22:31.941
Because even as siblings . . .

1307
01:22:31.941 --> 01:22:34.739
<v C. LATOUR>In spite of segregation, that’s how we were raised.

1308
01:22:34.739 --> 01:22:41.269
<v LATOUR-PITRE>And even in the household as siblings, if we started saying, “She said so and so, he said so and so.”

1309
01:22:41.269 --> 01:22:44.378
It was very much like, “Well, then, who are you?”

1310
01:22:44.378 --> 01:22:45.000
<v C. LATOUR>Yeah.

1311
01:22:45.000 --> 01:22:49.949
<v LATOUR-PITRE>So I think that transcended to being able to deal with racism as well.

1312
01:22:49.949 --> 01:22:52.071
And I tell my grandchildren that.

1313
01:22:52.071 --> 01:22:59.848
I said, “Once you adopt an attitude of who you are, and let them know that you’re here and that you’re present,”

1314
01:22:59.848 --> 01:23:04.091
my grandsons have Native American blood on both their other grandparents’ side.

1315
01:23:04.091 --> 01:23:10.808
It’s like, “If you know where you belong and you know you’re here, you don’t have to prove anything.”

1316
01:23:10.808 --> 01:23:13.283
You don’t have to . . . [phone rings] Okay.

1317
01:23:13.283 --> 01:23:13.990
I’m sorry.

1318
01:23:13.990 --> 01:23:16.465
I probably should have turned this off.

1319
01:23:16.465 --> 01:23:20.354
<v C. LATOUR>[ . . . ?] when you heard that phone. [ . . . ?] [laughter] It’s history for you, huh?

1320
01:23:20.354 --> 01:23:24.949
<v ALEXIS>I mean, it’s interesting, because I carried that and I wasn’t raised here.

1321
01:23:24.949 --> 01:23:26.717
You know what I mean?

1322
01:23:26.717 --> 01:23:29.545
I carried that confidence that they taught me.

1323
01:23:29.545 --> 01:23:30.606
<v LATOUR-PITRE>It’s a culture.

1324
01:23:30.606 --> 01:23:33.081
<v ALEXIS>I mean, I was always going here.

1325
01:23:33.081 --> 01:23:34.848
You know what I mean?

1326
01:23:34.848 --> 01:23:36.616
I still carry that [confidence?].

1327
01:23:36.616 --> 01:23:38.384
<v LATOUR-PITRE>It’s a cultural, and I . . .

1328
01:23:38.384 --> 01:23:42.626
<v ALEXIS>If somebody ask me what I am, I’m proud that I’m Creole.

1329
01:23:42.626 --> 01:23:44.394
I’m happy to say that.

1330
01:23:44.394 --> 01:23:44.747
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

1331
01:23:44.747 --> 01:23:46.869
And that’s the thing I recognize . . .

1332
01:23:46.869 --> 01:23:55.000
<v ALEXIS>People don’t understand that proudness . . . people don’t understand it, so to hear somebody explain it’s like oh, that’s why I’m like that.

1333
01:23:55.000 --> 01:24:01.290
<v LATOUR-PITRE>And that’s the thing that you realize, and that’s why I talk a lot, too, about my culture.

1334
01:24:01.290 --> 01:24:04.435
Because we do . . . we come from a Creole culture.

1335
01:24:04.435 --> 01:24:06.532
We come from a French culture.

1336
01:24:06.532 --> 01:24:09.677
And that’s what the culture teaches on all sides.

1337
01:24:09.677 --> 01:24:17.715
My mother’s family was a proud, proud but not arrogant, wise, didn’t fall for anything very easily, but didn’t get pushed around, neither.

1338
01:24:17.715 --> 01:24:19.812
I don't think any of them.

1339
01:24:19.812 --> 01:24:24.355
My grandfather, you’ll see my grandmother on my mother’s side and my grandfather.

1340
01:24:24.355 --> 01:24:25.753
He was very dark.

1341
01:24:25.753 --> 01:24:27.151
And she was biracial.

1342
01:24:27.151 --> 01:24:29.247
But he didn’t get pushed around.

1343
01:24:29.247 --> 01:24:30.296
No, didn’t happen.

1344
01:24:30.296 --> 01:24:34.489
<v C. LATOUR>There’s a certain sense of pride from generation to generation to generation.

1345
01:24:34.489 --> 01:24:40.430
There’s a certain sense of pride that we come up with, on both sides of our family.

1346
01:24:40.430 --> 01:24:40.780
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

1347
01:24:40.780 --> 01:24:41.828
But not arrogant.

1348
01:24:41.828 --> 01:24:43.226
<v C. LATOUR>Not arrogant at all.

1349
01:24:43.226 --> 01:24:43.575
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Humble.

1350
01:24:43.575 --> 01:24:45.323
They were a humble people.

1351
01:24:45.323 --> 01:24:47.769
And I try to remember that, too.

1352
01:24:47.769 --> 01:24:51.613
Because I think sometimes your attitude might imply that you’re arrogant.

1353
01:24:51.613 --> 01:24:53.710
I’ve been accused of being arrogant.

1354
01:24:53.710 --> 01:24:56.855
But it’s like no, it is what I am.

1355
01:24:56.855 --> 01:25:00.000
But I’m humble. [laughs] I try to be.

1356
01:25:00.000 --> 01:25:06.321
<v DRAGOON>Do you know how far back your . . . How far back do you know of your family history?

1357
01:25:06.321 --> 01:25:10.782
Did your parents or grandparents tell you all stories about their parents?

1358
01:25:10.782 --> 01:25:13.385
<v LATOUR-PITRE>They were here before the Civil War.

1359
01:25:13.385 --> 01:25:18.590
Because some of the family left and went to Haiti before the Civil War.

1360
01:25:18.590 --> 01:25:20.449
<v C. LATOUR>They weren’t ever slaves, though.

1361
01:25:20.449 --> 01:25:21.192
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Marguerite was.

1362
01:25:21.192 --> 01:25:23.051
<v C. LATOUR>Yes, she was a slave.

1363
01:25:23.051 --> 01:25:25.282
<v LATOUR-PITRE>And then she got her freedom.

1364
01:25:25.282 --> 01:25:29.000
And then she got the freedom for her children.

1365
01:25:29.000 --> 01:25:30.028
<v DRAGOON>And you said . . .

1366
01:25:30.028 --> 01:25:36.194
<v LATOUR-PITRE>She’s the only one that we know for sure we can link to . . . coming from a slave family.

1367
01:25:36.194 --> 01:25:37.907
There may be some others.

1368
01:25:37.907 --> 01:25:41.676
But we’ve done, made efforts to try to track the family.

1369
01:25:41.676 --> 01:25:47.843
And she was the only one, my grandfather’s side, she was the only one we can think of.

1370
01:25:47.843 --> 01:25:50.926
<v C. LATOUR>Going far back on Daddy’s side of the family.

1371
01:25:50.926 --> 01:25:54.694
<v LATOUR-PITRE>On the LaTours and the [Gradny?], the LaTours and the [Gradnys?]

1372
01:25:54.694 --> 01:25:59.491
<v C. LATOUR>The LaTours . . . I don't know which one was traced back to France and Italy.

1373
01:25:59.491 --> 01:26:03.259
I don't know whether it was the LaTours or the [Grandys?].

1374
01:26:03.259 --> 01:26:06.000
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Oh, the LaTours went back to France.

1375
01:26:06.000 --> 01:26:10.343
<v C. LATOUR>I know France, but I don't know if there was some that went back to Italy.

1376
01:26:10.343 --> 01:26:11.157
I don't know.

1377
01:26:11.157 --> 01:26:13.329
<v LATOUR-PITRE>The [Gradnys?] The [Gradnys?] go back to Italy.

1378
01:26:13.329 --> 01:26:14.686
My brother did a research.

1379
01:26:14.686 --> 01:26:16.857
And he and I kind of get it.

1380
01:26:16.857 --> 01:26:17.671
We do this.

1381
01:26:17.671 --> 01:26:19.571
We like to dig in this stuff.

1382
01:26:19.571 --> 01:26:25.000
So I don’t have the exact dates but yeah, he and I both tried to get, and it was interesting,

1383
01:26:25.000 --> 01:26:31.514
because there was again a family joke where for years and years and years my dad kept saying his family ran with the bulls,

1384
01:26:31.514 --> 01:26:34.500
which are people from Spain when they had the bull runs.

1385
01:26:34.500 --> 01:26:37.214
And he kept thinking he was, and he may still.

1386
01:26:37.214 --> 01:26:40.743
But he kept saying he was Spanish, he come from a Spanish family.

1387
01:26:40.743 --> 01:26:46.714
And when my brother start doing the genealogy and he started digging into it and it turns out [Gradnys?] were from Italy.

1388
01:26:46.714 --> 01:26:50.514
And the term they use here in Louisiana, you may have heard, is Dago.

1389
01:26:50.514 --> 01:26:53.771
I heard somebody the other day talking about someone was a Dago.

1390
01:26:53.771 --> 01:26:55.129
And I thought, oh, really?

1391
01:26:55.129 --> 01:26:58.114
But my brother started saying, “No, Dad, your family’s from Italy.

1392
01:26:58.114 --> 01:26:59.200
They’re not from Spain.”

1393
01:26:59.200 --> 01:27:00.557
And he was saying, “Italy!

1394
01:27:00.557 --> 01:27:00.829
Italy!”

1395
01:27:00.829 --> 01:27:07.614
I said, “Yeah, Daddy, he’s saying that your family was some Dagos.” [laughs] And he stopped and he looked at me like that he got,

1396
01:27:07.614 --> 01:27:14.400
he understood. [laughs] But yeah, so but like I said, I guess all of those people were here, the French from either Canada or France,

1397
01:27:14.400 --> 01:27:16.571
<c.p>from when they were doing that migration period.

1398
01:27:16.571 --> 01:27:17.386
And the LaTours.

1399
01:27:17.386 --> 01:27:19.557
And then the [Gradnys?] go way back, too.

1400
01:27:19.557 --> 01:27:21.186
I mean, 1600s, I would imagine.

1401
01:27:21.186 --> 01:27:22.000
For sure.

1402
01:27:22.000 --> 01:27:26.500
<v DRAGOON>Did you say that Marguerite moved to Haiti?

1403
01:27:26.500 --> 01:27:34.375
<v LATOUR-PITRE>No. Marguerite was owned by a family and started a relationship with the man . . .

1404
01:27:34.375 --> 01:27:36.062
<v C. LATOUR>The slave owner.

1405
01:27:36.062 --> 01:27:36.625
<v LATOUR-PITRE>[Pardon]?

1406
01:27:36.625 --> 01:27:38.312
<v C. LATOUR>The slave owner.

1407
01:27:38.312 --> 01:27:40.000
Her owner.

1408
01:27:40.000 --> 01:27:43.466
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Well the wife actually, she brought that to the marriage, supposedly.

1409
01:27:43.466 --> 01:27:49.452
When he married the wife . . . What I was reading, when he married the wife, the wife had the slaves.

1410
01:27:49.452 --> 01:27:54.178
So when they would get married, of course, the husband takes control of the estate.

1411
01:27:54.178 --> 01:27:56.699
So then he was overseer of the estate.

1412
01:27:56.699 --> 01:27:58.589
Had the relationship with my grandmother.

1413
01:27:58.589 --> 01:27:59.849
They had four children.

1414
01:27:59.849 --> 01:28:00.795
But he granted . . .

1415
01:28:00.795 --> 01:28:01.425
<v C. LATOUR>With Marguerite?

1416
01:28:01.425 --> 01:28:01.740
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

1417
01:28:01.740 --> 01:28:03.000
<v C. LATOUR>Five of them.

1418
01:28:03.000 --> 01:28:04.257
<v LATOUR-PITRE>They had several children.

1419
01:28:04.257 --> 01:28:05.514
<v DRAGOON>Marguerite was your grandmother?

1420
01:28:05.514 --> 01:28:05.829
Great?

1421
01:28:05.829 --> 01:28:07.086
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Was my grandfather’s grandmother.

1422
01:28:07.086 --> 01:28:07.400
<v DRAGOON>Oh.

1423
01:28:07.400 --> 01:28:08.657
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah, my grandfather’s grandmother.

1424
01:28:08.657 --> 01:28:10.857
So she’d be like a great-great-great grandmother.

1425
01:28:10.857 --> 01:28:12.429
<v C. LATOUR>His grandmother or his great-grandmother?

1426
01:28:12.429 --> 01:28:13.371
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Or his great-grandmother.

1427
01:28:13.371 --> 01:28:14.629
That’s what I said.

1428
01:28:14.629 --> 01:28:16.514
<v C. LATOUR>I think it was his great-grandmother.

1429
01:28:16.514 --> 01:28:18.086
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah, that’s what I’m saying.

1430
01:28:18.086 --> 01:28:20.286
<v C. LATOUR>Oh, I thought you said his grandmother.

1431
01:28:20.286 --> 01:28:22.800
<v LATOUR-PITRE>I say either his grandmother or his great-grandmother.

1432
01:28:22.800 --> 01:28:25.000
<v C. LATOUR>I think she was his great-grandmother.

1433
01:28:25.000 --> 01:28:27.355
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah, it could have been his great.

1434
01:28:27.355 --> 01:28:30.720
I think it was probably his great-grandmother . . . his father’s grandmother.

1435
01:28:30.720 --> 01:28:31.056
Yeah.

1436
01:28:31.056 --> 01:28:36.103
But the typical story where she had children with him, they developed apparently a relationship.

1437
01:28:36.103 --> 01:28:37.785
He gave her her freedom.

1438
01:28:37.785 --> 01:28:39.467
They moved to New Orleans.

1439
01:28:39.467 --> 01:28:46.196
He promised her the children’s freedom on his death, that they would allow them to be free when he died.

1440
01:28:46.196 --> 01:28:54.607
His white family and the wife and his children with her said the children were not free, that they were not going to be freed.

1441
01:28:54.607 --> 01:29:02.346
And these are in the records we have, the papers here, too, where she was in New Orleans and took it to court.

1442
01:29:02.346 --> 01:29:05.710
She went to court to try to free her children.

1443
01:29:05.710 --> 01:29:13.449
And it was settled with they were going to work for just so many years and then they would be allowed their freedom.

1444
01:29:13.449 --> 01:29:17.150
So she was able to get her freedom for her children.

1445
01:29:17.150 --> 01:29:23.542
So by the time my grandfather came along, they had been free people of color for a long time.

1446
01:29:23.542 --> 01:29:28.925
And they had acquired the land, I guess, under the Homestead Act and what have you.

1447
01:29:28.925 --> 01:29:32.290
And that’s the one link that we know for sure.

1448
01:29:32.290 --> 01:29:36.327
<v C. LATOUR>And the people that went to Haiti was . . . that was his grandfather.

1449
01:29:36.327 --> 01:29:37.000
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Right.

1450
01:29:37.000 --> 01:29:40.619
<v C. LATOUR>They went to Haiti during the war so they could remain free.

1451
01:29:40.619 --> 01:29:43.937
And then when it was all settled, then they came back.

1452
01:29:43.937 --> 01:29:44.238
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Right.

1453
01:29:44.238 --> 01:29:46.952
<v C. LATOUR>Nobody was actually from Haiti or born in Haiti.

1454
01:29:46.952 --> 01:29:49.968
Well, maybe some of the kids, but I don't know.

1455
01:29:49.968 --> 01:29:53.889
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Well there was a push . . . That was the other thing I read, too.

1456
01:29:53.889 --> 01:29:58.111
That the people, free people of color were trying to leave so they wouldn’t,

1457
01:29:58.111 --> 01:30:03.540
in case the South won the war, they were trying to leave because they figured they’d be re-enslaved.

1458
01:30:03.540 --> 01:30:03.841
<v C. LATOUR>Right.

1459
01:30:03.841 --> 01:30:06.556
<v LATOUR-PITRE>And in Louisiana, they were trying to encourage people.

1460
01:30:06.556 --> 01:30:11.984
They would run ads about, we’ll bankroll you to leave the state in case the North would win.

1461
01:30:11.984 --> 01:30:14.698
I guess they were afraid of rebellion and uprising.

1462
01:30:14.698 --> 01:30:20.730
So they were trying to get as many African slaves out of the country as possible, free people of color.

1463
01:30:20.730 --> 01:30:23.746
So some people took advantage of that and did go.

1464
01:30:23.746 --> 01:30:24.651
Some people stayed.

1465
01:30:24.651 --> 01:30:25.857
His family came back.

1466
01:30:25.857 --> 01:30:34.000
They decided to come back once they heard that the ending of the war, and that the South had not won the war, they came back.

1467
01:30:34.000 --> 01:30:37.932
<v DRAGOON>Do you know if you had family who fought in the war?

1468
01:30:37.932 --> 01:30:38.588
<v LATOUR-PITRE>I don’t.

1469
01:30:38.588 --> 01:30:45.797
Not long ago, I was listening to PBS and there was something about the English and the French when they were fighting,

1470
01:30:45.797 --> 01:30:48.090
was it the English in New Orleans?

1471
01:30:48.090 --> 01:30:50.712
The big battle that was in New Orleans.

1472
01:30:50.712 --> 01:30:54.644
And all of a sudden there was a mention about a General

1473
01:30:54.644 --> 01:30:59.559
<c.p>something LaTour that either provided information or was in one of the skirmishes or something.

1474
01:30:59.559 --> 01:31:05.130
And I wrote it down someplace with the intent of trying to go back and find it.

1475
01:31:05.130 --> 01:31:12.339
Actually, I think when I looked back to see if I could find it, they said he had never had a family.

1476
01:31:12.339 --> 01:31:15.616
He had never actually gotten married and had a family.

1477
01:31:15.616 --> 01:31:16.927
So I dropped that.

1478
01:31:16.927 --> 01:31:23.480
I figured maybe . . . My thing about being in Southwest Louisiana is that we’re all third degree cousins somewhere or another.

1479
01:31:23.480 --> 01:31:27.085
It doesn’t take you long before you find the connection. [laughs]

1480
01:31:27.085 --> 01:31:28.395
<v C. LATOUR>Right. [They’re our kin?].

1481
01:31:28.395 --> 01:31:32.000
<v LATOUR-PITRE>It’s like yeah, oh, yeah, that’s one of ours, too.

1482
01:31:32.000 --> 01:31:36.070
<v DRAGOON>I want to go back to the ‘60s for a second.

1483
01:31:36.070 --> 01:31:45.320
Or I guess fast forward to the ‘60s. [laughs] Do you have any memories of voter registration drives in the schools or anything like that?

1484
01:31:45.320 --> 01:31:51.240
<v LATOUR-PITRE>No, because like I say, I think when I got married, I was nineteen years old.

1485
01:31:51.240 --> 01:31:57.160
And I got married so young that when I got married and left then I voted,

1486
01:31:57.160 --> 01:32:01.600
I registered to vote, I didn’t have to experience the voter registration.

1487
01:32:01.600 --> 01:32:09.000
<v C. LATOUR>In the area, in Lake Charles, south of Mossville, over there, I don’t ever remember them having a problem.

1488
01:32:09.000 --> 01:32:11.846
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Because Mom and Dad would vote even when we were . . .

1489
01:32:11.846 --> 01:32:13.838
<v C. LATOUR>Our parents voted when we were kids.

1490
01:32:13.838 --> 01:32:14.123
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

1491
01:32:14.123 --> 01:32:19.531
<v C. LATOUR>So I don't remember in our area where we lived, unlike in Mississippi and Alabama and all of that.

1492
01:32:19.531 --> 01:32:19.815
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

1493
01:32:19.815 --> 01:32:22.377
<v C. LATOUR>And I’m not saying it didn’t happen in Louisiana.

1494
01:32:22.377 --> 01:32:24.369
But in the area where we lived . . .

1495
01:32:24.369 --> 01:32:29.492
<v LATOUR-PITRE>I think being land owners and maybe, I guess, there was the disadvantage of people didn’t own property,

1496
01:32:29.492 --> 01:32:32.623
I guess that was one of the bars they were using.

1497
01:32:32.623 --> 01:32:34.331
<v C. LATOUR>And there was always, always voting.

1498
01:32:34.331 --> 01:32:35.754
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah, but they would vote.

1499
01:32:35.754 --> 01:32:38.315
<v C. LATOUR>And people in Mossville would turn out to vote.

1500
01:32:38.315 --> 01:32:38.885
A lot.

1501
01:32:38.885 --> 01:32:40.308
I mean, they would vote.

1502
01:32:40.308 --> 01:32:42.300
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah, the community . . . yeah, they did vote.

1503
01:32:42.300 --> 01:32:44.577
They would have rallies and stuff like that.

1504
01:32:44.577 --> 01:32:46.000
So they did vote.

1505
01:32:46.000 --> 01:32:47.311
<v DRAGOON>What kinds of rallies?

1506
01:32:47.311 --> 01:32:48.951
Do you have any memories?

1507
01:32:48.951 --> 01:32:51.574
<v LATOUR-PITRE>We were too young to go to them.

1508
01:32:51.574 --> 01:32:56.492
But it would be like the hot dogs, come on out and eat and vote.

1509
01:32:56.492 --> 01:32:57.475
They’d have barbecues.

1510
01:32:57.475 --> 01:33:01.082
<v C. LATOUR>And they had, when the segregation started, then we had the . . .

1511
01:33:01.082 --> 01:33:01.738
<v LATOUR-PITRE>The integration?

1512
01:33:01.738 --> 01:33:05.016
<v C. LATOUR>We had the food thing . . . Was it Walgreen’s in downtown?

1513
01:33:05.016 --> 01:33:06.000
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Oh, yeah.

1514
01:33:06.000 --> 01:33:06.880
<v C. LATOUR>With the counter.

1515
01:33:06.880 --> 01:33:11.573
They used to have the counter in the store there was like a little food counter.

1516
01:33:11.573 --> 01:33:14.507
You could get ice cream and hot dogs and stuff.

1517
01:33:14.507 --> 01:33:17.147
Like a little café, I guess, you’d call it.

1518
01:33:17.147 --> 01:33:18.027
And strictly segregated.

1519
01:33:18.027 --> 01:33:23.893
Just like in those other states, if you’d go in that little place and they wouldn’t want to serve you,

1520
01:33:23.893 --> 01:33:26.827
that was a little, I guess the rally did that.

1521
01:33:26.827 --> 01:33:28.880
But even with that . . . I don't remember.

1522
01:33:28.880 --> 01:33:32.400
I have been turned away when I was at school at McNeese.

1523
01:33:32.400 --> 01:33:35.920
And there was a little hamburger joint across the street from McNeese.

1524
01:33:35.920 --> 01:33:38.560
A girlfriend of mine went to school with me.

1525
01:33:38.560 --> 01:33:41.200
She worked there in the back as a cook.

1526
01:33:41.200 --> 01:33:42.667
And she was telling me

1527
01:33:42.667 --> 01:33:42.960
<c.p>that.

1528
01:33:42.960 --> 01:33:47.947
I said okay, maybe one day for lunch I’ll walk across and grab a hamburger from school.

1529
01:33:47.947 --> 01:33:50.880
And me and another friend of mine was a guy.

1530
01:33:50.880 --> 01:33:52.640
And it was John’s friend Earl.

1531
01:33:52.640 --> 01:33:55.280
I don't know if you remember Earl, John’s friend.

1532
01:33:55.280 --> 01:33:55.867
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Not clearly.

1533
01:33:55.867 --> 01:33:59.973
<v C. LATOUR>He and I went across the street and when we got to the door,

1534
01:33:59.973 --> 01:34:05.253
the people came and stood at the door and told us they didn’t serve the “N” word there.

1535
01:34:05.253 --> 01:34:07.307
So we wasn’t, we couldn’t go in.

1536
01:34:07.307 --> 01:34:10.533
My girlfriend worked there, but we couldn’t go in and eat.

1537
01:34:10.533 --> 01:34:12.293
And that was in ‘65, ‘66?

1538
01:34:12.293 --> 01:34:14.640
Because I graduated from high school in ‘65.

1539
01:34:14.640 --> 01:34:14.933
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

1540
01:34:14.933 --> 01:34:15.227
Yeah.

1541
01:34:15.227 --> 01:34:16.987
<v C. LATOUR>You know, that kind of stuff.

1542
01:34:16.987 --> 01:34:19.627
<v LATOUR-PITRE>It was in the height, during that period, too.

1543
01:34:19.627 --> 01:34:19.920
<v C. LATOUR>Yeah.

1544
01:34:19.920 --> 01:34:21.973
It was just that kind of stuff.

1545
01:34:21.973 --> 01:34:22.853
There wasn’t like . . .

1546
01:34:22.853 --> 01:34:26.080
<v LATOUR-PITRE>It was a time when it was really rocking and rolling.

1547
01:34:26.080 --> 01:34:32.240
<v C. LATOUR>And another time when I went to church, we was told the same thing at, what’s the big church right there?

1548
01:34:32.240 --> 01:34:34.000
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Our Lady, Queen of Heaven?

1549
01:34:34.000 --> 01:34:34.780
<v C. LATOUR>Queen of Heaven.

1550
01:34:34.780 --> 01:34:38.940
When we pulled up in the parking lot and a car pulled up right behind us,

1551
01:34:38.940 --> 01:34:42.580
blocking us in and told us the “N” word, “Don’t go to church there.”

1552
01:34:42.580 --> 01:34:44.400
So those kind of things like that.

1553
01:34:44.400 --> 01:34:45.960
And then you pick your battles.

1554
01:34:45.960 --> 01:34:50.120
It’s like, okay, how important is it for me to be in the church parking lot?

1555
01:34:50.120 --> 01:34:55.840
And if it’s just the two of us, and it’s like, okay, we’re not fixing to do anything, so you just leave.

1556
01:34:55.840 --> 01:34:56.620
You just leave.

1557
01:34:56.620 --> 01:35:01.300
<v LATOUR-PITRE>And that was the thing, I think, that we were taught as a kid in the community, maybe,

1558
01:35:01.300 --> 01:35:07.540
was that like you don’t . . . Nobody never really encouraged us to like pick up the banners and go down the street and do rah,

1559
01:35:07.540 --> 01:35:08.840
rah, rah, that I remember.

1560
01:35:08.840 --> 01:35:09.880
We didn’t do that.

1561
01:35:09.880 --> 01:35:13.520
But in a quiet, determined way, one step at a time, it was like,

1562
01:35:13.520 --> 01:35:18.200
there was a determination that things were going to change and that we were going to do this.

1563
01:35:18.200 --> 01:35:23.660
But not to the level, the risk that some of the other people took and paid with their lives for it.

1564
01:35:23.660 --> 01:35:26.000
So in our case, it was more like . . .

1565
01:35:26.000 --> 01:35:27.942
<v C. LATOUR>And I think we had to do that.

1566
01:35:27.942 --> 01:35:30.612
Because like when I watch what they would do on TV,

1567
01:35:30.612 --> 01:35:35.709
when we would see them with the protestors with the dogs, and then they would hose them down with the water.

1568
01:35:35.709 --> 01:35:38.864
The biggest commotion we had where there was a situation with the cops,

1569
01:35:38.864 --> 01:35:43.233
and people were standing all around and wanting to fight, was in Westlake, when they integrated Westlake School.

1570
01:35:43.233 --> 01:35:43.476
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

1571
01:35:43.476 --> 01:35:45.417
Westlake got a little rough, I remember hearing.

1572
01:35:45.417 --> 01:35:46.874
Because I was gone by then.

1573
01:35:46.874 --> 01:35:47.845
<v C. LATOUR>It was very bad.

1574
01:35:47.845 --> 01:35:49.301
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Because I was gone by then.

1575
01:35:49.301 --> 01:35:51.000
But I understand they got crazy.

1576
01:35:51.000 --> 01:35:52.166
<v C. LATOUR>It was extremely bad.

1577
01:35:52.166 --> 01:35:54.790
Kids were being threatened and police would come out.

1578
01:35:54.790 --> 01:35:57.705
I don't know who was the officials at that time.

1579
01:35:57.705 --> 01:36:02.952
But they were like . . . make it perfectly clear that I guess we going to have to do this,

1580
01:36:02.952 --> 01:36:07.033
because that’s what the federal law say, but this is not what we want.

1581
01:36:07.033 --> 01:36:07.908
It was bad.

1582
01:36:07.908 --> 01:36:10.240
And every day these kids were at school . . .

1583
01:36:10.240 --> 01:36:10.531
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

1584
01:36:10.531 --> 01:36:14.030
See, I was gone by then, but I remember hearing the stories.

1585
01:36:14.030 --> 01:36:14.321
Yeah.

1586
01:36:14.321 --> 01:36:15.196
Those kids were . . .

1587
01:36:15.196 --> 01:36:17.819
<v C. LATOUR>And Westlake was the only one that did that.

1588
01:36:17.819 --> 01:36:20.443
Because everybody, whether Sulphur wanted them there or not . . .

1589
01:36:20.443 --> 01:36:23.066
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Sulphur had a small scrimmage, but not so bad.

1590
01:36:23.066 --> 01:36:28.314
<v C. LATOUR>They just kind of let them oh well, we don’t want you all here, but come on. [laughs]

1591
01:36:28.314 --> 01:36:38.517
<v LATOUR-PITRE>That’s the example I was telling you about when a kid gets to school and the first thing you’ve got to deal with is the fear of whether you’re going to get hurt or not.

1592
01:36:38.517 --> 01:36:43.764
That makes it kind of hard to settle in and say okay, I’m here for the right reasons.

1593
01:36:43.764 --> 01:36:44.930
They stay in a

1594
01:36:44.930 --> 01:36:46.096
<c.p>constant state of fear.

1595
01:36:46.096 --> 01:36:47.554
Makes it a lot harder.

1596
01:36:47.554 --> 01:36:51.343
And that’s true, actually transcend not just from the schools. [laughs] Okay, sidebar.

1597
01:36:51.343 --> 01:36:58.048
I think it still, I think it’s still something most black people, most of us as a minority have to be concerned about.

1598
01:36:58.048 --> 01:36:58.923
It still is.

1599
01:36:58.923 --> 01:37:02.129
Even when you walk into restaurants and you get weird looks.

1600
01:37:02.129 --> 01:37:05.627
Like I always get that feeling like what are you doing here?

1601
01:37:05.627 --> 01:37:07.668
It’s like oh, lord, here we go.

1602
01:37:07.668 --> 01:37:08.542
It’s 2015, people.

1603
01:37:08.542 --> 01:37:10.000
We’re here. [laughs] So.

1604
01:37:10.000 --> 01:37:19.903
<v DRAGOON>What do you remember about MLK, Martin Luther King, Junior, being on the national stage in your time during that period?

1605
01:37:19.903 --> 01:37:22.261
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Again, I was already married.

1606
01:37:22.261 --> 01:37:26.977
My mother took an extreme sense of pride in that.

1607
01:37:26.977 --> 01:37:31.693
And [whipped out?] her curiosity, you’ve got to see this.

1608
01:37:31.693 --> 01:37:38.295
Like I said, she was full on and supportive in the way she could,

1609
01:37:38.295 --> 01:37:45.369
and encouraged us to recognize how important it was, and to appreciate what was happening.

1610
01:37:45.369 --> 01:37:49.142
That’s kind of what I can think about.

1611
01:37:49.142 --> 01:37:55.273
And then of course by the time he was killed, I was married.

1612
01:37:55.273 --> 01:38:01.403
And my husband called me and told me that he had been shot.

1613
01:38:01.403 --> 01:38:09.892
But the “I have a dream” march and speech, I remember her watching that and talking about it.

1614
01:38:09.892 --> 01:38:12.722
And so we were all in.

1615
01:38:12.722 --> 01:38:22.625
At the level we were, we were all in. [laughs] We could certainly see the benefit and the need for it.

1616
01:38:22.625 --> 01:38:23.097
Yeah.

1617
01:38:23.097 --> 01:38:33.000
And like I said, I guess our education had prepared us to believe that we could make a difference. [laughs]

1618
01:38:33.000 --> 01:38:37.690
<v DRAGOON>I’m going to change tracks just a little bit.

1619
01:38:37.690 --> 01:38:46.028
Did you have family who served in the military, World War I or World War II?

1620
01:38:46.028 --> 01:38:47.592
<v C. LATOUR>Two. [unclear] uncles.

1621
01:38:47.592 --> 01:38:49.676
<v LATOUR-PITRE>We had Uncle Frank.

1622
01:38:49.676 --> 01:38:52.803
He didn’t actually see active duty.

1623
01:38:52.803 --> 01:38:54.887
He was in Florida.

1624
01:38:54.887 --> 01:38:56.451
<v C. LATOUR>Uncle [Bailey?] did.

1625
01:38:56.451 --> 01:38:56.972
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

1626
01:38:56.972 --> 01:39:00.620
These are two of my mother’s brothers.

1627
01:39:00.620 --> 01:39:02.704
<v C. LATOUR>[Uncle Bailey did?] Oh, [?]

1628
01:39:02.704 --> 01:39:03.225
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

1629
01:39:03.225 --> 01:39:07.394
Two of her brothers we know for sure.

1630
01:39:07.394 --> 01:39:10.000
<v C. LATOUR>Did Uncle [Bailey?] serve?

1631
01:39:10.000 --> 01:39:12.456
<v LATOUR-PITRE>That’s what I was trying to remember.

1632
01:39:12.456 --> 01:39:14.211
And what about Uncle Wilford?

1633
01:39:14.211 --> 01:39:15.263
Didn’t Uncle Wilford?

1634
01:39:15.263 --> 01:39:17.719
I’m not sure about my dad’s family.

1635
01:39:17.719 --> 01:39:20.175
I’m not sure about my dad’s family.

1636
01:39:20.175 --> 01:39:25.088
I think if there was, it maybe was just one of them who served.

1637
01:39:25.088 --> 01:39:31.053
But the two that we know who were actively, were involved, that that we can remember is,

1638
01:39:31.053 --> 01:39:35.614
and then we had my, one of her brother-in-laws also served, Uncle Roy.

1639
01:39:35.614 --> 01:39:38.070
He served big time in the Pacific.

1640
01:39:38.070 --> 01:39:41.930
And Uncle Alec was in the European side of the war.

1641
01:39:41.930 --> 01:39:46.491
And the typical behaviors or stories you hear about when they come back.

1642
01:39:46.491 --> 01:39:48.947
Cool guys but . . . [laughs] kind of edgy.

1643
01:39:48.947 --> 01:39:55.263
And so, and like I said, by the time the war ended, I wasn’t even born yet, so . . .

1644
01:39:55.263 --> 01:39:58.421
<v DRAGOON>Did your parents talk about that time for them?

1645
01:39:58.421 --> 01:40:00.877
<v LATOUR-PITRE>No. Because I think again, their lifestyle.

1646
01:40:00.877 --> 01:40:02.982
They were out in the fields.

1647
01:40:02.982 --> 01:40:06.140
And that’s the thing I recognize, too, about wars.

1648
01:40:06.140 --> 01:40:13.158
When I would look at the Vietnam War and these people out in the rice paddies and I’m going like,

1649
01:40:13.158 --> 01:40:17.368
that’s what people really want to do is just grow their rice.

1650
01:40:17.368 --> 01:40:21.228
They don’t really . . . And everybody is creating chaos all around them.

1651
01:40:21.228 --> 01:40:26.491
And I guess they were sheltered and they did their daily life and their routines.

1652
01:40:26.491 --> 01:40:28.596
And they were aware of it.

1653
01:40:28.596 --> 01:40:30.702
Like a lot of rural America.

1654
01:40:30.702 --> 01:40:36.667
Unless they had a son that was really involved, they were aware of what was going on.

1655
01:40:36.667 --> 01:40:40.877
And then like I said, when they moved to the Mossville area,

1656
01:40:40.877 --> 01:40:47.544
Maplewood was being developed and the refineries were really, had finally starting to break loose and was being developed.

1657
01:40:47.544 --> 01:40:53.509
So they started working for people who had come down to them for that boom, I guess.

1658
01:40:53.509 --> 01:40:56.316
And found jobs as a result of that.

1659
01:40:56.316 --> 01:41:01.228
And I would think that’s probably what brought them to this area, huh, Cil?

1660
01:41:01.228 --> 01:41:02.982
The post-war jobs that was . . .

1661
01:41:02.982 --> 01:41:03.333
<v C. LATOUR>Yeah.

1662
01:41:03.333 --> 01:41:05.789
To get away from where they were.

1663
01:41:05.789 --> 01:41:06.491
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Well, yeah.

1664
01:41:06.491 --> 01:41:10.000
That was being created by the refineries and stuff.

1665
01:41:10.000 --> 01:41:13.864
<v DRAGOON>What do you remember about the refineries when you were growing up?

1666
01:41:13.864 --> 01:41:15.153
<v LATOUR-PITRE>They were scary. [laughs]

1667
01:41:15.153 --> 01:41:17.407
<v C. LATOUR>And they had a lot of explosions.

1668
01:41:17.407 --> 01:41:18.695
<v LATOUR-PITRE>A lot of explosions.

1669
01:41:18.695 --> 01:41:21.271
For me, literally living in a state of,

1670
01:41:21.271 --> 01:41:28.034
because you would hear some of the explosions and things that could happen and how bad it was going to be.

1671
01:41:28.034 --> 01:41:33.831
For me it was, yeah, I can remember being afraid of living within the shadows of . . . And again,

1672
01:41:33.831 --> 01:41:41.237
we were kind of in an area where we were almost kind of isolated or protected from some of the really bad stuff.

1673
01:41:41.237 --> 01:41:42.847
But yeah, a definite concern.

1674
01:41:42.847 --> 01:41:48.000
And when the explosions would go off, the house would just rattle. [laughs] It’s like . . .

1675
01:41:48.000 --> 01:41:49.075
<v DRAGOON>What would happen?

1676
01:41:49.075 --> 01:41:54.448
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Something would blow, a tank or something like that, and it would shake the neighborhood.

1677
01:41:54.448 --> 01:41:56.597
The house, the windows would rattle.

1678
01:41:56.597 --> 01:42:00.179
I don't think we ever had any that actually broke.

1679
01:42:00.179 --> 01:42:02.328
But they would rattle pretty good.

1680
01:42:02.328 --> 01:42:06.627
And then you’d start hearing that dreadful siren that would go off.

1681
01:42:06.627 --> 01:42:11.642
And when you had family working there, then everybody, then you’re holding your breath.

1682
01:42:11.642 --> 01:42:20.955
Until you hear from your family member, you’re holding your breath because you didn’t know exactly where it is or what plant it might have been.

1683
01:42:20.955 --> 01:42:22.388
The siren’s going off.

1684
01:42:22.388 --> 01:42:28.478
So I can remember a few times, yeah, we were sitting around holding our breath and waiting.

1685
01:42:28.478 --> 01:42:34.925
I guess one of the last times when we were impacted was when Phil was at PP, no . . .

1686
01:42:34.925 --> 01:42:35.284
<v C. LATOUR>Citgo?

1687
01:42:35.284 --> 01:42:36.000
Coneco?

1688
01:42:36.000 --> 01:42:36.625
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah, Coneco.

1689
01:42:36.625 --> 01:42:39.750
Was it phosgene . . . was it phosgene gas that had escaped?

1690
01:42:39.750 --> 01:42:41.312
Was it a gas escape?

1691
01:42:41.312 --> 01:42:43.188
<v C. LATOUR>I don't know what it was.

1692
01:42:43.188 --> 01:42:44.438
I don't know why.

1693
01:42:44.438 --> 01:42:46.938
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Because I wasn’t living here at the time.

1694
01:42:46.938 --> 01:42:48.188
I heard the story.

1695
01:42:48.188 --> 01:42:52.250
But it was some kind of toxic release and he was working there.

1696
01:42:52.250 --> 01:42:53.812
No, there was an explosion.

1697
01:42:53.812 --> 01:42:57.250
I remember he said he felt the . . . there was an explosion.

1698
01:42:57.250 --> 01:43:02.875
<v C. LATOUR>He was right near, because when he opened the door, right where he was there was a fire.

1699
01:43:02.875 --> 01:43:04.750
I don't know what it caused.

1700
01:43:04.750 --> 01:43:05.062
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

1701
01:43:05.062 --> 01:43:07.250
There was an explosion and a fire.

1702
01:43:07.250 --> 01:43:10.062
So that was, yeah, that was in the ‘70s.

1703
01:43:10.062 --> 01:43:12.562
So that was one of the last times.

1704
01:43:12.562 --> 01:43:20.688
And again, being a kid and then leaving the area, I had heard, and I think they have benefited the community overall in terms of jobs,

1705
01:43:20.688 --> 01:43:30.688
being able to create jobs for people in the community is probably another reason why we thrive and little places like Mossville was able to stay alive was because of the jobs.

1706
01:43:30.688 --> 01:43:32.250
But everything has a tradeoff.

1707
01:43:32.250 --> 01:43:36.000
Some of those jobs and those conditions were kind of rough.

1708
01:43:36.000 --> 01:43:39.000
<v DRAGOON>What kinds of jobs were people taking there?

1709
01:43:39.000 --> 01:43:39.750
<v LATOUR-PITRE>For minorities?

1710
01:43:39.750 --> 01:43:42.375
I think mostly labor kind of jobs.

1711
01:43:42.375 --> 01:43:43.125
Manual labor.

1712
01:43:43.125 --> 01:43:44.625
My father got lucky.

1713
01:43:44.625 --> 01:43:48.375
He was working with people who taught him a craft.

1714
01:43:48.375 --> 01:43:50.250
But for the most part,

1715
01:43:50.250 --> 01:44:02.250
the craft that was learned by some of the men who were working there was people who just either needed it or was kind enough to show them how to do things.

1716
01:44:02.250 --> 01:44:09.000
And in their own determination to learn to do it well . . . And then they became specialized at it.

1717
01:44:09.000 --> 01:44:10.125
And that did

1718
01:44:10.125 --> 01:44:13.500
<c.p>happen for quite a few men who worked there.

1719
01:44:13.500 --> 01:44:17.250
But that was the one thing about segregation back then.

1720
01:44:17.250 --> 01:44:24.000
It wasn’t exactly like oh, we all love everybody and open arms and you’re welcome to come in.

1721
01:44:24.000 --> 01:44:28.875
If you got lucky enough to get in, and in my dad’s case,

1722
01:44:28.875 --> 01:44:33.375
how did he, how did he . . . oh, Mom worked for Mr. [Mount?].

1723
01:44:33.375 --> 01:44:36.000
Somebody told me that the other day.

1724
01:44:36.000 --> 01:44:39.750
My mother was housekeeper for this man and his family.

1725
01:44:39.750 --> 01:44:43.500
And she was talking about Dad being out of work.

1726
01:44:43.500 --> 01:44:47.625
And the man said, “Well, have him come talk to me.

1727
01:44:47.625 --> 01:44:48.750
Firestone is hiring.”

1728
01:44:48.750 --> 01:44:58.125
So when he talked to the man, the man made it possible for my dad to get an interview and get the job at Firestone.

1729
01:44:58.125 --> 01:45:04.875
So when you got lucky like that, then you could get in and would get a good job.

1730
01:45:04.875 --> 01:45:13.500
Other than that, he’d have been, every rainy day he’d have been sitting on that back porch, watching what we were doing. [laughs]

1731
01:45:13.500 --> 01:45:16.500
<v C. LATOUR>[Never?] did that. [Never?] got him [distracted?]. [laughter]

1732
01:45:16.500 --> 01:45:21.750
<v LATOUR-PITRE>No, he made sure we got on the track to . . . “Oh, you sitting here?

1733
01:45:21.750 --> 01:45:24.000
You ain’t got nothing to do?”

1734
01:45:24.000 --> 01:45:28.500
<v DRAGOON>What were some of the other places that folks in Mossville worked?

1735
01:45:28.500 --> 01:45:31.875
So a lot of them worked at the plants.

1736
01:45:31.875 --> 01:45:33.000
And were there . . .

1737
01:45:33.000 --> 01:45:35.625
<v C. LATOUR>That was the majority of it, really.

1738
01:45:35.625 --> 01:45:37.125
<v LATOUR-PITRE>And the construction people.

1739
01:45:37.125 --> 01:45:39.000
They would do construction work.

1740
01:45:39.000 --> 01:45:45.000
If somebody was building something, then they would go out and do that kind of thing.

1741
01:45:45.000 --> 01:45:45.375
Lumber

1742
01:45:45.375 --> 01:45:45.750
<c.p>mills.

1743
01:45:45.750 --> 01:45:50.625
I think they had a mill that was still running for a while.

1744
01:45:50.625 --> 01:45:54.750
Had a couple of them in Mossville who were little entrepreneurs.

1745
01:45:54.750 --> 01:45:57.750
Most of it was whatever they could get.

1746
01:45:57.750 --> 01:46:04.125
I failed to tell you, too, what an avid fisher and hunter my father was. [laughs] Okay.

1747
01:46:04.125 --> 01:46:04.875
Back step.

1748
01:46:04.875 --> 01:46:06.000
Go ahead.

1749
01:46:06.000 --> 01:46:07.912
<v DRAGOON>Now I wanted to know more.

1750
01:46:07.912 --> 01:46:09.504
<v LATOUR-PITRE>He loved fishing and hunting.

1751
01:46:09.504 --> 01:46:10.779
<v DRAGOON>Where would he go?

1752
01:46:10.779 --> 01:46:15.558
<v LATOUR-PITRE>As a matter of fact, when he got to be old, a little bit older,

1753
01:46:15.558 --> 01:46:19.699
after he was working for a while, he got himself a little boat.

1754
01:46:19.699 --> 01:46:23.204
And he would tell you a story about his shrimp boats.

1755
01:46:23.204 --> 01:46:25.752
Like no, you just got one little pirogue.

1756
01:46:25.752 --> 01:46:29.894
Here in the lakes . . . Big Lake, he would go out to Big Lake.

1757
01:46:29.894 --> 01:46:33.717
And the channels through here, he would go and fish in there.

1758
01:46:33.717 --> 01:46:35.947
And then somewhere they would find rivers.

1759
01:46:35.947 --> 01:46:39.133
My grandfather, Joe, his father, was an avid fisherman, also.

1760
01:46:39.133 --> 01:46:41.044
<v DRAGOON>Did they ever sell their catches?

1761
01:46:41.044 --> 01:46:42.000
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Oh, no.

1762
01:46:42.000 --> 01:46:42.383
<v DRAGOON>No.

1763
01:46:42.383 --> 01:46:48.506
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Everything that they got their hands on and they brought it [around?] . . . because like I said,

1764
01:46:48.506 --> 01:46:55.395
income wasn’t such that they could just . . . So they supplemented their income with foods and gardens a lot.

1765
01:46:55.395 --> 01:46:58.457
It was a way of life for them.

1766
01:46:58.457 --> 01:47:01.136
This is what they were used to.

1767
01:47:01.136 --> 01:47:03.432
<v DRAGOON>Could we get a time check?

1768
01:47:03.432 --> 01:47:04.963
See where we are.

1769
01:47:04.963 --> 01:47:05.346
Okay.

1770
01:47:05.346 --> 01:47:05.728
Cool.

1771
01:47:05.728 --> 01:47:06.494
All right.

1772
01:47:06.494 --> 01:47:09.173
So we’re at an hour fifty, about.

1773
01:47:09.173 --> 01:47:14.914
I have a couple more questions and then maybe we can wrap up this session.

1774
01:47:14.914 --> 01:47:15.296
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Okay.

1775
01:47:15.296 --> 01:47:17.593
<v DRAGOON>I have many pages of questions.

1776
01:47:17.593 --> 01:47:18.741
So many. [laughs]

1777
01:47:18.741 --> 01:47:21.420
<v LATOUR-PITRE>We can do it in another part.

1778
01:47:21.420 --> 01:47:24.864
Yeah, we can do it in another part. [laughter]

1779
01:47:24.864 --> 01:47:25.247
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

1780
01:47:25.247 --> 01:47:27.160
<v LATOUR-PITRE>You guys are good company.

1781
01:47:27.160 --> 01:47:29.457
We can pick you up later.

1782
01:47:29.457 --> 01:47:30.988
<v DRAGOON>We can come back.

1783
01:47:30.988 --> 01:47:31.370
Okay.

1784
01:47:31.370 --> 01:47:32.136
Let’s see.

1785
01:47:32.136 --> 01:47:35.580
I just wanted to know, did your parents cook?

1786
01:47:35.580 --> 01:47:37.111
Did your mom cook?

1787
01:47:37.111 --> 01:47:38.642
Did your dad cook?

1788
01:47:38.642 --> 01:47:39.025
<v C. LATOUR>[Pardon]?

1789
01:47:39.025 --> 01:47:40.556
<v LATOUR-PITRE>[laughs] Yes and yes.

1790
01:47:40.556 --> 01:47:44.000
<v DRAGOON>Both of them, or just one of them?

1791
01:47:44.000 --> 01:47:44.762
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yes and yes.

1792
01:47:44.762 --> 01:47:49.844
My father and I, one of our things . . . That was another thing, too, about the pecking order in the house.

1793
01:47:49.844 --> 01:47:51.623
Each older child was responsible for something.

1794
01:47:51.623 --> 01:47:53.656
The oldest male was responsible for man things.

1795
01:47:53.656 --> 01:47:58.230
He had to know how to fix a car, and you got to know how to do yard.

1796
01:47:58.230 --> 01:48:01.025
You got to know how to take care of your house.

1797
01:48:01.025 --> 01:48:03.820
The oldest female had to know how to do house things.

1798
01:48:03.820 --> 01:48:05.598
You got to be able to cook.

1799
01:48:05.598 --> 01:48:10.934
One of the biggest fights we would have when I was trying to learn how to cook was cooking that rice.

1800
01:48:10.934 --> 01:48:12.459
He would go ballistic, the rice.

1801
01:48:12.459 --> 01:48:15.762
So finally one day he showed me, “I’m going to show you something.

1802
01:48:15.762 --> 01:48:17.287
This is how you cook rice!”

1803
01:48:17.287 --> 01:48:20.844
Because it was always too soggy, or it wasn’t water, didn’t have enough water.

1804
01:48:20.844 --> 01:48:23.639
And when he showed me the one-inch trick with your finger.

1805
01:48:23.639 --> 01:48:27.959
“When you put the water in pot, you put your finger in there and you measure that.

1806
01:48:27.959 --> 01:48:31.262
And if it’s right there, then . . . ” To this day, I’ll die with that.

1807
01:48:31.262 --> 01:48:32.279
But hey, it works.

1808
01:48:32.279 --> 01:48:32.787
It works.

1809
01:48:32.787 --> 01:48:34.566
So, yeah, they were definitely both cooks.

1810
01:48:34.566 --> 01:48:37.869
After I was gone, he would throw these big cookouts for you guys.

1811
01:48:37.869 --> 01:48:40.918
He would have all you all come to the house and cook.

1812
01:48:40.918 --> 01:48:43.459
And he’d be the one who would be cooking, right?

1813
01:48:43.459 --> 01:48:43.713
<v C. LATOUR>Yeah.

1814
01:48:43.713 --> 01:48:45.492
He’d make sauce most of the time.

1815
01:48:45.492 --> 01:48:46.000
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

1816
01:48:46.000 --> 01:48:47.850
<v C. LATOUR>He’d make some kind of sauce.

1817
01:48:47.850 --> 01:48:49.392
That’s what he would do.

1818
01:48:49.392 --> 01:48:51.858
They would boil crawfish or something like that.

1819
01:48:51.858 --> 01:48:55.867
But mostly what he . . . the best thing he cooked was like those sauces.

1820
01:48:55.867 --> 01:48:56.175
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

1821
01:48:56.175 --> 01:48:58.025
<v C. LATOUR>The fish and stuff like that.

1822
01:48:58.025 --> 01:48:58.333
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Turtles.

1823
01:48:58.333 --> 01:48:58.642
Fish.

1824
01:48:58.642 --> 01:49:00.492
He would go get the turtles.

1825
01:49:00.492 --> 01:49:07.892
And when he would clean them, he’d give us those little eggs that was in there. [laughs] My husband tease me all the time.

1826
01:49:07.892 --> 01:49:10.050
If it don’t move, we eat it.

1827
01:49:10.050 --> 01:49:10.667
<v C. LATOUR>Oh, yeah.

1828
01:49:10.667 --> 01:49:11.283
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Love turtle.

1829
01:49:11.283 --> 01:49:11.900
Love sheep.

1830
01:49:11.900 --> 01:49:14.983
Love pork, chicken, rabbit, you name it. [laughter] But again,

1831
01:49:14.983 --> 01:49:20.225
because of the way they fed us, that’s what they helped us develop a palate for food.

1832
01:49:20.225 --> 01:49:23.000
I mean, if it’s cooked right, it’s good.

1833
01:49:23.000 --> 01:49:25.362
<v DRAGOON>Did they pass recipes on to you?

1834
01:49:25.362 --> 01:49:26.374
<v LATOUR-PITRE>No. No recipes.

1835
01:49:26.374 --> 01:49:28.736
Just this is how you make it.

1836
01:49:28.736 --> 01:49:30.423
<v C. LATOUR>They didn’t have a recipe.

1837
01:49:30.423 --> 01:49:31.098
Just cook.

1838
01:49:31.098 --> 01:49:32.785
<v LATOUR-PITRE>There’s another inside joke again.

1839
01:49:32.785 --> 01:49:34.810
Margaret boiled vegetables in the microwave.

1840
01:49:34.810 --> 01:49:35.485
Cecelia cooks.

1841
01:49:35.485 --> 01:49:38.859
<v C. LATOUR>She is the recipe book. [laughs] She remembered it all.

1842
01:49:38.859 --> 01:49:43.245
<v LATOUR-PITRE>It is not that I don't know how to cook and can’t cook.

1843
01:49:43.245 --> 01:49:45.270
And I think you like it.

1844
01:49:45.270 --> 01:49:45.945
<v C. LATOUR>I do.

1845
01:49:45.945 --> 01:49:47.969
<v LATOUR-PITRE>I tease her all the time.

1846
01:49:47.969 --> 01:49:48.982
She’s the foodie.

1847
01:49:48.982 --> 01:49:51.344
She’ll take pictures of foods and stuff.

1848
01:49:51.344 --> 01:49:52.356
She likes food.

1849
01:49:52.356 --> 01:49:53.706
She likes preparing food.

1850
01:49:53.706 --> 01:49:55.055
She likes preparing food.

1851
01:49:55.055 --> 01:49:58.092
She’ll call us sometime and say, “Come and eat.”

1852
01:49:58.092 --> 01:49:59.442
And it’s like, okay.

1853
01:49:59.442 --> 01:50:01.129
<v C. LATOUR>Yeah, I like to cook.

1854
01:50:01.129 --> 01:50:03.491
I like to cook for a crowd.

1855
01:50:03.491 --> 01:50:05.515
I have a big family, too.

1856
01:50:05.515 --> 01:50:09.227
I had, with my own kids, I have a big family.

1857
01:50:09.227 --> 01:50:10.914
So I like to cook.

1858
01:50:10.914 --> 01:50:14.626
I don’t cook like I used to as much. [baby crying]

1859
01:50:14.626 --> 01:50:15.638
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Poor little [Pichi?]

1860
01:50:15.638 --> 01:50:18.000
<v C. LATOUR>I hope I cook like Dad.

1861
01:50:18.000 --> 01:50:20.154
<v LATOUR-PITRE>What you need, Mama?

1862
01:50:20.154 --> 01:50:23.385
That sounds like I need something.

1863
01:50:23.385 --> 01:50:23.923
Nap?

1864
01:50:23.923 --> 01:50:24.462
Milk?

1865
01:50:24.462 --> 01:50:25.000
Something.

1866
01:50:25.000 --> 01:50:28.231
<v C. LATOUR>I need that dog on me.

1867
01:50:28.231 --> 01:50:32.000
<v ALEXIS>That’s what he want, that dog. [laughter]

1868
01:50:32.000 --> 01:50:34.692
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Go look in the bedroom.

1869
01:50:34.692 --> 01:50:40.077
<v ALEXIS>He wants her, because he think she just so pretty.

1870
01:50:40.077 --> 01:50:43.308
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah, I know, I can see.

1871
01:50:43.308 --> 01:50:46.000
Go look in the bedroom.

1872
01:50:46.000 --> 01:50:48.692
There’s stuffed animals in there.

1873
01:50:48.692 --> 01:50:51.923
Open that closet door. [baby noises]

1874
01:50:51.923 --> 01:50:53.000
<v DRAGOON>So cute.

1875
01:50:53.000 --> 01:50:54.615
<v LATOUR-PITRE>I’m working here.

1876
01:50:54.615 --> 01:50:56.769
Don’t start with me.

1877
01:50:56.769 --> 01:51:02.154
I don’t have time to play with you right now.

1878
01:51:02.154 --> 01:51:07.000
Let me finish. [laughs] You can go ahead.

1879
01:51:07.000 --> 01:51:12.247
<v DRAGOON>Did you guys have big family gathering meals when you were growing up? [laughter] Was that like a nightly or a weekly kind of basis?

1880
01:51:12.247 --> 01:51:13.506
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Once or twice a month, maybe.

1881
01:51:13.506 --> 01:51:13.926
<v C. LATOUR>At least.

1882
01:51:13.926 --> 01:51:16.444
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Once a month, for sure, if you want to average it out.

1883
01:51:16.444 --> 01:51:17.074
We was always . . .

1884
01:51:17.074 --> 01:51:18.123
<v C. LATOUR>At least once a month.

1885
01:51:18.123 --> 01:51:19.173
<v LATOUR-PITRE>And I didn’t live here.

1886
01:51:19.173 --> 01:51:19.593
<v C. LATOUR>Or more.

1887
01:51:19.593 --> 01:51:20.222
Once or twice.

1888
01:51:20.222 --> 01:51:20.642
Oh, yeah.

1889
01:51:20.642 --> 01:51:21.481
<v LATOUR-PITRE>It’s like oh, yeah.

1890
01:51:21.481 --> 01:51:23.580
Food was one of the big things that we did.

1891
01:51:23.580 --> 01:51:24.000
<v C. LATOUR>Still.

1892
01:51:24.000 --> 01:51:30.024
<v LATOUR-PITRE>I was looking at the pictures the other day about the backyard when I was putting the pictures of the property together.

1893
01:51:30.024 --> 01:51:33.310
And I go like yeah, I need to include some of these.

1894
01:51:33.310 --> 01:51:34.679
Because the backyard was busy.

1895
01:51:34.679 --> 01:51:36.321
We always had gatherings and kids.

1896
01:51:36.321 --> 01:51:36.869
<v C. LATOUR>Oh, yeah.

1897
01:51:36.869 --> 01:51:38.238
We did that a lot.

1898
01:51:38.238 --> 01:51:39.333
We still do that.

1899
01:51:39.333 --> 01:51:39.881
Mm hmm.

1900
01:51:39.881 --> 01:51:40.155
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

1901
01:51:40.155 --> 01:51:40.429
Yeah.

1902
01:51:40.429 --> 01:51:41.250
We still do.

1903
01:51:41.250 --> 01:51:42.893
<v C. LATOUR>Because I have a big family.

1904
01:51:42.893 --> 01:51:44.262
My kids are grown now.

1905
01:51:44.262 --> 01:51:47.274
And we have cookouts at their house and stuff like that.

1906
01:51:47.274 --> 01:51:50.012
Last Saturday we was cooking out. [laughs] Yeah, we do.

1907
01:51:50.012 --> 01:51:50.833
Very much so.

1908
01:51:50.833 --> 01:51:52.476
<v LATOUR-PITRE>And we feed off of her.

1909
01:51:52.476 --> 01:51:53.845
We live vicariously through her.

1910
01:51:53.845 --> 01:51:55.762
Just about all the rest of us.

1911
01:51:55.762 --> 01:51:57.405
<v C. LATOUR>It’s part of the culture, though.

1912
01:51:57.405 --> 01:51:59.595
I mean, and it carries everybody, the culture.

1913
01:51:59.595 --> 01:52:00.690
Because everybody’s like that.

1914
01:52:00.690 --> 01:52:06.714
No matter where we are in the country, if you came from this family, you use food to bring the family together.

1915
01:52:06.714 --> 01:52:07.810
That’s how it is.

1916
01:52:07.810 --> 01:52:08.083
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Absolutely.

1917
01:52:08.083 --> 01:52:09.452
<v C. LATOUR>And it brings everybody together.

1918
01:52:09.452 --> 01:52:10.000
So.

1919
01:52:10.000 --> 01:52:10.936
<v LATOUR-PITRE>And it’s noisy!

1920
01:52:10.936 --> 01:52:15.929
I have literally lost my voice trying to talk over these people when we’ve been together.

1921
01:52:15.929 --> 01:52:16.241
Literally.

1922
01:52:16.241 --> 01:52:17.801
The next day, I’m hoarse.

1923
01:52:17.801 --> 01:52:20.609
Because for some reason, my pitch level doesn’t work.

1924
01:52:20.609 --> 01:52:22.793
I’m screaming, and everybody else is fine.

1925
01:52:22.793 --> 01:52:26.226
I have literally lost my voice more than once trying to . . .

1926
01:52:26.226 --> 01:52:28.722
<v C. LATOUR>You’ve got to get in when you finish.

1927
01:52:28.722 --> 01:52:29.034
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

1928
01:52:29.034 --> 01:52:29.346
Yeah.

1929
01:52:29.346 --> 01:52:32.466
<v C. LATOUR>I probably have the biggest family of all of them.

1930
01:52:32.466 --> 01:52:39.019
So when we, just my kids and their children, and their children’s children, I think we right at 35 right now.

1931
01:52:39.019 --> 01:52:44.947
<v LATOUR-PITRE>The family joke is if you want to throw a party and you need to know how many people,

1932
01:52:44.947 --> 01:52:49.316
just count sixty-five people for Nanny Celia, and then see who else you have.

1933
01:52:49.316 --> 01:52:52.436
You can kind of say, Celia’s got the largest group.

1934
01:52:52.436 --> 01:52:55.244
And like I said, we live vicariously through her.

1935
01:52:55.244 --> 01:52:57.741
Because I have two children with four grandchildren.

1936
01:52:57.741 --> 01:53:03.981
One grandson lives here with me, and a son’s in the area, the rest of my kids . . . So it’s like,

1937
01:53:03.981 --> 01:53:08.662
there have been many holidays we’re sitting here like just the two of us, okay.

1938
01:53:08.662 --> 01:53:11.782
My oldest brother, he’s got two children and one grandchild.

1939
01:53:11.782 --> 01:53:13.342
Just the two of them.

1940
01:53:13.342 --> 01:53:18.959
So when they get together, it’s like, “Can we come play with you all and you all children?”

1941
01:53:18.959 --> 01:53:23.015
So we have the big family, so fortunately we get invited a lot.

1942
01:53:23.015 --> 01:53:24.887
But a lot of family gatherings.

1943
